<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Market Strategy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Market Strategy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Market Strategy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Marketing Strategy Management Aggregated Function groups functions useful to define the marketstrategy (existing and desired market-place, activities and aims, objectives to be achieved, market segmentation) and to develop market, channels.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Market Sales and Channel Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Market Sales and Channel Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Market Sales and Channel Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Market and Channel are developed to support new or enhanced products.
The Market and Channel Development function comprises predictions defining product sales forecasts and targets for each channel, as well as sales performance indicators for each channel and geomarketing analysis.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Market and Channel Strategy Definition -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Market and Channel Strategy Definition">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Market and Channel Strategy Definition</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">A Market and Channel Strategy Definition is established, from a Market and Channel analysis, in respect with the strategic business direction.
It formalizes, in a Market Strategy Plan, the Market and Channel key stakes, strategic axes and multi years objectives for the company, as well as Market and Channel objectives, segmentation and targeting, positioning.
It includes: 
- forecasts of the Market and Channel sales. Forecasts are carried out from a chronological modeling of the tendencies, the forecasts of commercial actions or marketing.
- identification of market segment, customer profile and channels used across the enterprise
- definition of behaviors models and strategy:
     - development of purchase behavior models that will be used for the marketing strategy development, in order to improve the distribution performance. These models will be used for the statistical tool implementation (customer profile type) and the creation of statistical data (dashboards).
     - a broad Sales Strategy to support the market strategy with a Sales model management defining the various stages of the sales pipeline, which can be based on the SPANCO method (Suspect qualification, Prospect qualification, Analyzing, Negotiating, Closing, Ordering).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Plan Definition -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Plan Definition">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Marketing Campaign Plan Definition</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Marketing Campaign Plan Definition Aggregate Function deals with the marketing campaign plan definition to manage communications to the market, prospective and existing customers, to suggest options to them. 

	- it defines a whole of campaigns from marketing studies and plan, plans these campaigns
	- it optimizes the contacts according to campaign prioritization rules such as the potential creation of value of the proposed offer

It concerns mainly:

	- The identification of the campaigns that should be executed to reach the business objectives for a specific customer segment and their sequence (campaign name, description, campaign type, objectives, cost, sponsor, planning)
	- The contact rules definition with targeting criterion to make sure that commercial pressure on customer is managed (e.g. "no more than 1 SMS per week for customer segment 1")
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Market Information Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Market Information Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Market Information Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Market Information Management Aggregate Function allows to collect information from external or internal sources, to obtain a better knowledge of the market and competitors.
The information collected may concern, for example:
- market, market shares, general trends, potentials,
- customer and consumer expectations and motivations, new modes of consumption,
- distribution, general trends, new distribution channels
- competition, competitors, their market shares and strategy (positioning, target, etc.),
- environment: politic, economic (cartography of the geo-merchandising zones), technologic, ecological, legislative
- enterprise image & fame, etc.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Marketing Strategic Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Marketing Strategic Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Marketing Strategic Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Marketing Strategic Analysis Aggregated Function groups functions useful to conduct delayed analysis, on the collected information concerning market, which may have an influence on the strategy.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Marketing Campaign Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Campaign Analysis consists in the assessment of the actions and the targeting returns in order to evaluate the generated added value (campaign effectiveness, ROI, effectiveness of the channels and teams, cost of the actions (time, budget), relevance or not of the actions carried out) and optimize the next marketing campaigns.
Evaluates the campaigns performance:
- At the end of the campaign. Proposition take rate will be considered, as well as campaigns impact on usage, top-up behavior (increase in amount/frequency) and churn rate
- In the long run. E.g., has the retention campaign had a durable impact on churn reduction, or has it triggered opportunistic customer behavior? Has the stimulate usage campaign caused cannibalization, or triggered a durable increase in consumption?</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Market and Channel Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Market and Channel Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Market and Channel Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Market and Channel Analysis Aggregate Function concerns analysis of the market and channel with a market and customer view.
Analyses rely on market &amp; channel research, information gathering, watch, studies.
A market and channel research consists in evaluating the market potentials and anticipates the competition strategy (analysis of the market shares and competition and evolution assumptions).

Analysis concern both external and internal elements knowledge:

External elements knowledge:
- on market, knowledge of the market shares by region with general trends, potentials in volume, in CA,
- on customer and consumer, expectations and motivations, new modes of consumption, calculation of the customer average revenues according to the offer range and flow (acquisition, churn, migration),
- on distribution, general trends, new distribution channel,
- on competition, anticipate the competition strategy (analysis of the market shares and competition and evolution assumptions, positioning, target, social network verbatim analysis),
- on environment: politic, economic (cartography of the geo-merchandising zones), technologic, ecological, legislative.

Internal elements knowledge:
- on Company Performances, turnover evolution, profitability, financial means,
- on customer, enterprise image &amp; fame, qualification of the customers (loyalty, satisfaction, contact mode, frequency, income, purchasing power, equipment, verbatim on social networks), purchase behavior,
- market shares by region, penetration rates of the offers by region, number of eligible lines by region, etc.
It also take into account assessment of the Strategy performance results in order to improve the Market Strategy.

It includes geomarketing functions which start from the sociological assumption according to which the habitat choice is a consumption reasoned act, largely conditioned by financial and social constraints, leading to specific tendencies of consumption. The geomarketing detects the potential values of new life zones (Mass Market: life zones, Business Market: new attractive industrial / commercial areas).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Marketing Campaign Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Marketing Campaign Development Aggregated Function groups operational functions useful to define campaigns. There are different campaigns types, direct marketing campaigns, but also information campaigns (by examples information about the migrations, newsletters, etc.).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Design -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Design">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Marketing Campaign Design</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Marketing Campaign Design Aggregate Function concerns the phase of the preparation of a defined campaign in a multichannel environment to deliver one-to-one or right-time campaigns. It establishes the targeted customers list and/or criteria, the proposition, visuals, waves, planning and metrics to evaluate the campaign. It extracts the target list to prepare the execution on channels and provides the campaign brief.

- the targeted customers list definition is based both on the criteria held in the Campaign Definition function and on the analysis of the existing data (e.g. analytics) or of predictive modeling for a specific campaign. The targeting takes into account the management of the commercial pressure from the event history of the proactive and reactive campaigns and the events of contacts concerning the targeted customers. 
- new type of campaigns uses the geofencing infrastructure to push messages, promotions such as coupons, advertising, to customers when they enter or leave a designated area and are localized by beacons. These campaigns need to define content push rules and personal context, and to check customer opt-in.
- the proposition that will be addressed to each customer sub-segment is described in terms of generosity, requested effort and validity period, and event triggers.
- the visuals supporting the message (mailing &amp; e-mailing channels) are created 
- the campaign is split in waves, each of them is associated with a sales channel and a communication mode.
- the campaigns business case, metrics and KPIS are defined as well. 
- the campaign message definition
- and eventually policy rules definition according to event-based marketing capabilities to trigger automated campaigns.

The main outputs of this function are the campaign brief, mix and metrics.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Workflow Design -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Workflow Design">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Marketing Campaign Workflow Design</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Marketing Campaign Workflow Design Aggregate Function is in charge of designing Campaign Worflows and manage its approvals.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Commission Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Commission Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Commission Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sales Commission Management Aggregated Function groups functions specifying commission rules for the Service Provider's employees such as Sales Agent.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Commission Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Commission Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Commission Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sales Commission Development Aggregate Function defines the revenue forecasts, sales representative objectives and the sales force's received compensation for sales based on performance against quotas assigned
It enables the follow-up of sales representatives forecasts / objectives, sales trends</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Advertising Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Advertising Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Advertising Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Advertising Management AF includes functions supporting planning, executing, and overseeing advertising campaigns to promote products or services. Effective advertising management plays a crucial role in building brand awareness, driving sales, and achieving marketing objectives. Its key activities include setting campaign objectives, identifying target audiences, selecting appropriate advertising channels, creating compelling ad content, budgeting, monitoring campaign performance, and adjusting strategies as needed. The goal is to maximize the reach and impact of advertising while ensuring efficient resource allocation.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Advertisement Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Advertisement Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Advertisement Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Advertisement Management AF groups functions that address the lifecycle of advertisements (whether they are actually used or not), from concept through creation through testing through placement, effectiveness assessment, and retirement.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Marketing Campaign Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Marketing Campaign Management Aggregated Function groups operational functions useful to conduct campaign, etc.).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Execution -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Execution">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Marketing Campaign Execution</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Marketing Campaign Execution function consists in executing and driving marketing campaign through the different channels. 

Campaign execution is launched by performing interactions with customers or prospects and may include scripts execution. It collects answers through different media.
It takes in input the campaign brief, the support to use, script if necessary, and the customer target list or criteria from the campaign design function. 
Example of criteria: send specific notifications or alerts (promotions, advertising, ) when a customer (/prospect/user) enters or leaves a designated area (based on geofencing infrastructure - and customer opt-in)

The campaign execution can involve: 
- distribution of marketing collateral like leafleting, displays at events, inserts in newspapers, television and other media advertising and magazines or direct marketing through store.
- a voice or text messaging (SMS, mail, call ) for a targeted customer list.
- a document push such as coupon, photo or video 

Campaign Execution provides the necessary tools to execute and refine the campaign, and based on performance indicators collected, accept refinements to the campaign while still executing.
Campaign test is the first phase of campaign execution (specific waves, limited audience). Its restricted execution to validate campaign.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Performance Tracking and Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Marketing Campaign Performance Tracking and Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Marketing Campaign Performance Tracking and Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Marketing Campaign Performance Tracking and Analysis provides the necessary functionality to monitor the campaign performance (e.g. performance indicators) as the campaign is being executed. 
It permits to continuously optimize campaigns.
This is done by providing a combination of real-time reporting and what-if modeling capabilities to continuously optimize campaigns by taking corrective action-even when campaigns are already underway. 
Functionality specifically includes:
- Real-time view into customizable segmentation schemes and key metrics. 
- Dashboard views, alerts, and notifications provide marketing organizations with the insight and agility needed to protect and optimize marketing investments. 
- Pre-built Marketing Insight reports.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Commissioning Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Commissioning Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Commissioning Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Sales Commissioning Management Aggregated Function encompasses several functionalities:
- the definition of commissioning rules for the sellers, dealers or resellers
- commissions calculation for sellers, dealers or resellers from information such as orders, activation, usage, etc. applying commission rules</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Account Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Account Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Account Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sales Account Management Aggregate Function is in charge of defining Sales Accounts to sellers assigning customers for products and activities.
Assignment might be done analyzing Sales Opportunities. It also provides a view of the service provider's relationship with the customer.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Commission Calculation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Commission Calculation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Commission Calculation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Sales Commission Calculation function consists in calculating commissions for sellers, dealers or resellers from information such as orders, activation, usage, etc.
The calculation is based on rules defined by the Sales Management or by the distribution agreement.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Commission Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Commission Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Commission Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sales Commission Repository Management Aggregated Function manages Sales Commission inventory and provides reports.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales and Sales Force Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales and Sales Force Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales and Sales Force Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Sales and Sales Force Management Aggregate Function encompasses several functionalities:
- Sales Territory Management enables to structure the sales teams into territories according to several criteria: business segments, strategic accounts, product lines, industries, geographical areas The territories are used for the lead / opportunity assignation and the sales forecasts.
- Sales Force Management manages the direct (Customer Service Representatives - CSR - in call centers and shops) and indirect (dealers, resellers, outsourcers) sales forces. It defines the revenue forecasts, sales representative objectives and enables the follow-up of sales representatives forecasts / objectives, sales trends
It consists in planning their activities, their visits with customers as well as some indicators about their efficiency in achieving the goals of a marketing campaign. It encompasses the objectives management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Territory Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Territory Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Territory Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sales Territory Management Aggregate Function enables to structure the sales teams into territories according to several criteria: business segments, strategic accounts, product lines, industries, geographical areas The territories are used for the lead / opportunity assignation and the sales forecasts.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Force Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Force Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Force Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sales Force Management Aggregate Function manages the direct (Customer Service Representatives - CSR - in call centers and shops) and indirect (dealers, resellers, outsourcers) sales forces. It defines the revenue forecasts, sales representative objectives and enables the follow-up of sales representatives forecasts / objectives, sales trends
It consists in planning their activities, their visits with customers as well as some indicators about their efficiency in achieving the goals of a marketing campaign. It encompasses the objectives management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sales Management Aggregated Function groups functions in charge of processing the proposition, negotiation and sales towards a customer interested in an offer or product.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Lead Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Lead Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Lead Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Sales Lead Management Aggregate Function enables to collect, evaluate and attribute the leads to the appropriate sales representatives.
The lead is an interest concerning an offer family or a product line, or expressed literally during an interaction, possibly further to a campaign, and it must be handled and specified to become an opportunity or an order.
A lead can be generated from many different sources and customer interactions including the result of a targeted marketing campaign. Potential customer information is obtained from external sources or from internally generated data.
A prospect could have expressed a commercial interest or a commercial interest has been detected from his behavior analysis.
The territories, defined by the Sales and Sales Force Management function are used for the lead assignation.

It also manages relationships between leads and various campaigns and tracks the quality of leads by noting which leads are converted to actual sales after being worked.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Opportunity Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Opportunity Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Opportunity Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Opportunity Management Aggregate Function enables to collect, qualify the opportunities and supervise their conversion into orders.
Qualify the opportunity first includes to convert the leads into opportunities.
Once a lead is qualified, it is considered as an opportunity, which is worked further down the sales funnel through to either success (sale) or loss/disqualification (no sale).
An opportunity can be generated from many different sources and customer interactions including the result of a targeted marketing campaign. An opportunity is linked with the Offer &amp; Product Catalogue.
All information necessary to make an offer to the Prospective Customer are gathered. Potential customer information is obtained from external sources or from internally generated data.
It could also manage relationships between opportunities and various campaigns.
The opportunity may be further explored with the Prospective Customer (extended interactions) to ensure the customer requirement is understood.
It includes also Proposal Management part which enables the conversion of Opportunities into agreed proposal. 
A solution is elaborated, proposed to the Prospective Customer and his acceptation / refusal is collected. There may be a negotiation.
If the Prospective Customer accepts the commercial proposition one or several Order will be generated via the Customer Order Management functions.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Interlocutors Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Interlocutors Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Interlocutors Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Sales Interlocutors Management Aggregate Function permits to identify CSP and Customer information and organization, in the context of Opportunity Management or Framework Agreement Management. It mainly concerns B2B customers.

It can contain:
- The attribution to the appropriate sales representatives, according to the territories defined by the Workforce Management function
- The identification and the authorization of the customer company employees (to purchase, to add service, to obtain support, ...), 
- The way invoices and/or invoicing reports will be handled in the future (for each order, each site of the enterprise, ) via the Billing Administration function,</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Framework Agreement Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Framework Agreement Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Framework Agreement Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Framework Agreement Management Aggregate Function consists in defining and managing the agreement that describes the commitments and company features valid for associated customer orders.
It defines a subset of catalogue offers and products which will be marketed to a customer with particular conditions (configurations of product, rates, discounts, SLA such as availability rate, restoration time guaranties, and associated penalties for the CSP).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Performance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Performance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Performance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sales Performance Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of measuring and reporting sales performance.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sales Analysis Aggregate Function is in charge of Sales performance measurement according to pre-defined metrics rules.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sales Reporting -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sales Reporting">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sales Reporting</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sales Reporting Aggregate Function is in charge of Sales reporting.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Market and Channel Performance Tracking -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Market and Channel Performance Tracking">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Market and Channel Performance Tracking</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Market and Channel Performance Tracking provides the necessary functionality to monitor the performance, on market and channel axes. 
It includes: 

	- Market and channel performance measurement,
	- Follow-up of the performances and profitability of the market and channels (reporting concerning the performances and the impacts of the marketing, sales, new customers acquisitions).
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Analysis Aggregate Function groups functions useful to conduct delayed analysis, on the collected information concerning products such as product usages or product usages, which may have an influence on the strategy.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Profitability Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Profitability Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Profitability Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Profitability Analysis Aggregate Function calculates Products Profitability based on Product's cost and profits.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Usage Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Usage Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Usage Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Usage Analysis Aggregate Function comprises:
- Analysis of the customers' usages with identification and quantification of the products little used and even not used.
- Forecast of the usages with predictions of the product uses with a chronological modeling of the tendencies and possible breaks of consumption, in order to be compliant with the company strategy (impacts: financial, marketing, trade, network).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop provides the necessary functionalities to manage a quality loop on diagnostic to continually tweak and improve it.
This function provides an offline analysis of the usage of the tests to determine its relevance and provide modifications concerning the quota and authorized users. This analysis is done using statistical test usage data to evaluate the tests relevance and the needed updates.
Following the analysis, the creation of additional product tests can be proposed or a test can be considered irrelevant and deleted.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product QoS Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product QoS Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product QoS Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product QoS Analysis Aggregate Function comprises:

	- Steering of the quality loop: Development of a global "Dashboard" in order to analyze the quality / efficiency of the service provided to the customers, measure the customer satisfaction and analyze their claims and/or troubles.

Statistics concerning the customer satisfaction and Quality of Service: Development of a report in order to analyze the customer satisfaction in terms of QoS (QoS as seen from the customer viewpoint), from the indicators concerning the main processes :  Customer Welcome, Customer Inquiries, Support to the service use, Claims and Complaints, After Sales, Interaction Channels. These statistics are also calculated for the set of installed products owned by the customer,  to determine the customer satisfaction score.

	- Aggregate analysis of QoS performance across groups of customers or aggregate analysis of SLA types.
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Strategy Definition & Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Strategy Definition & Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Specification & Offering Strategy Definition & Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Specification &amp; Offering Strategy Definition &amp; Analysis defines the overall structure of the offers and products portfolios to be used across the enterprise. It is established, from the Offer and Product analysis, in respect with the strategic business direction and covers new offers and products as well as existing offers and products. This Aggregated Function also groups functions related to Product Specification &amp; Offering Strategy Analysis conduct.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Strategy Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Strategy Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Specification & Offering Strategy Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Specification &amp; Offering Strategy Analysis Aggregate Function rely on offer and product research, external and internal information gathering, watch, studies on the market evolution.
An Offer and Product market research consists in evaluating the market potentials for an Offer or Product and anticipates the competition strategy (analysis of the concurrent offers and products, tariff simulations).

Analysis concern both external and internal elements knowledge taking into account assessment of the strategy performance results in order to improve the Offer and Product Strategy and campaign results.

External elements knowledge:
- on consumer, expectations and motivations toward a product, measure of customer satisfaction,
- on competition, concurrent products, tariff simulations, price, promotion, etc.
- on environment, politic, economic, technologic, ecological, legislative.

Internal elements knowledge:
- market shares by product, offer performance, penetration rates of the offers by region, number of eligible lines by region,
- actualization of the forecast model concerning the revenues,
- analysis of the revenues and costs (estimated, consolidated, trend, and their composition according to the offers, parts of the traffic)
- analysis of the market with an offer and product view including geomarketing functions in order to optimize the use of the existing network with a logic of Yield Management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Strategy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Strategy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Specification & Offering Strategy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Specification &amp; Offering Strategy Management Aggregate Function formalizes, in a Market Strategy Plan, the Offer and Product key stakes, strategic axes and multi years objectives for the company, as well as the Offer and Product Portfolio Business Plans with offer and product (Pricing strategies, Costs, Forecasts, targets), place, promotion.

It comprises :
- Offer and Product portfolio business plan: This plan includes portfolio forecasts, anticipated pricing strategies, projected revenue and costs, product.
- Forecasts of the Offer and Product sales: Forecasts are carried out from a chronological modeling of the tendencies, the forecasts of commercial actions or marketing.
- Product Marketing Promotion Strategy: specific communications and promotions strategy is defined for positioning the offer and product in the marketplace. It defines a set of arguments and information needed for creating and developing specific promotional campaigns for each offer and product.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Specification & Offering Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Specification &amp; Offering Management Aggregate Function Functions enable to create and update the catalog. This function covers the impact on the offer and product catalogue of new offers and products launches, modifications and withdrawals. This function manages the lifecycle of offers and products and includes the various possibilities of offer or product search and browse (wizard, multicriterion search, category browse).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Ordering Rules Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Ordering Rules Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Ordering Rules Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Ordering Rules Development Aggregate Function is in charge of defining and managing business rules used for ordering. It contains rules like cross/up sell rules, eligibility rules, customer fraud check, order duplication prevention rules, etc.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Specification & Offering Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Specification &amp; Offering Development Aggregate Function enables to create and update the catalogue. This function covers the impact on the offer and product catalogue of new offers and products launches, modifications and withdrawals. This function manages the lifecycle of offers and products and includes the various possibilities of offer or product search and browse (wizard, multicriterion search, category browse).

At the Offer level, the catalogue describes:
- packaging rules of the contract: mandatory offers, optional offers, offers that can be ordered in number (ex: 1 to 4 mobile lines)
- commercial incompatibilities or pre-requisites (ex: necessary to be the holder of a X contract to subscribe Y contract)
- available commercial operations (ex: contract migration)
- available commitment durations
- any commercial criteria such as authorized sales channel or geographic area, customer criteria, 
- tariff specifications - and possible alterations. They are associated to the offer, to commercial operations or usage types and can be recurring or one shot. They are expressed as rules that can take into account many criteria (ex: commitment duration, product configuration, sales channel, customers age, ) and will be evaluated during the order capture process, or during the rating process for usage.

At the Product level, for every product commercialized through one or several offers, the catalogue describes:
- characteristics of the product, and their possible values (ex: speed, volume, duration, phone number, )
- available functional operations (ex: change of speed)
- functional incompatibilities or pre-requisites (deducted from CFS specification incompatibilities or pre-requisites)
- link with the know-how type (CFS specification) from which the intangible product is a restriction (ex: mobile line, VOIP, ), or directly with the Stock Item for tangible products (ex: smartphone, SIM Card), or to the Supplier product type in case of purchase products.

The Product Catalogue describes all the tangible and intangible products that can be commercialized through standard offers, loyalty offers.
Example:
- goodies can be sold or offered as a reward in exchange of fidelity points (same product commercialized through 2 offers)
- a special discount can be granted through a retention offer
- etc.

The Offers or Products described in the catalogue can be associated to different types of documentation:
- sales leaflet, standard and specific sales terms
- photos or videos
- QR codes, that can be sent to the customer or available on a poster, in a magazine,  to ease access to any type of catalogue documents
ratings and reviews from customers or CSP experts
- etc.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Test Specification Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Test Specification Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Test Specification Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Test Specification Development Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage the product test catalogue containing the product test specifications, the rules that define the test configuration setting up based on the context, the rules that define the strategies for executing the test as well as how the test results should be restituted.
Test strategies can range from simple to complex and encompass the definition of test scenario.
The definition of the test (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the question that is asked and thus shapes the result that is reported back.
The functionalities include providing:

	- Product Test Specification management

  - The Product Test Specification is defined, depending on the atomic product specification, based on a Service Test Specification or directly a Resource Test Specification (for example for a resource like a box, an STB)
  - Managing product test capabilities: the underlying service or resource tests used to define the product test have limitation that must be taken into account. Additional limitations can be enforce at the product level especially for the self-tests for the users.
  - Managing initiator authorization: some tests are restricted to some user roles (internal or external) so the authorization are defined at the Product Test Strategy and Policy Management

	- Test rules defining the strategies for setting up test configuration that is necessary for executing the service or resource test on which the product test is based


	- Test rules defining the restitution of the result depending on test execution context

  - For example: a test result of ok/not ok can be enough for a user whereas customer care may requires more details in the result of the test.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Realization -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Specification & Offering Realization">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Specification & Offering Realization</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Specification & Offering Realization AF groups functions involved in bringing Product Specifications to market in a smooth and coordinated fashion.  These functions generally bridge functional and organizational boundaries.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Specification Lifecycle Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Specification Lifecycle Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Specification Lifecycle Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Specification Lifecycle Management AF includes functions that handle the lifecycle changes (from conception through retirement) of Product Specifications, whether internally-developed and provided or created by one or more Business Partner independently or in conjunction with the Service Provider, and offered by the Service Provider or provided either by a Business Partner via the Service Provider..

These functions provide the necessary functionality to onboard or incorporate a partner's prospective product(s) into the service provider's product catalog or "store front", the partner's store front, etc. This functionality also includes the capability to manage the partner's product lifecycle from inception to retirement. External products can include tangible and intangible products based on resources, services and applications.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Supervision Policy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Supervision Policy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Supervision Policy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">TBA</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of managing the repository of installed Products corresponding as well to ProductSpecification or ProductOffering and all related information.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Loyalty Account Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Loyalty Account Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Loyalty Account Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Loyalty Account Management Aggregate Function is specific to Loyalty Products which imply to count advantages in units such as points. It encompasses:
- Loyalty account management: enables the consultation of the balances, the follow-up of points usage, and validity periods.
- Earn module: it enables to trigger the Loyalty Actions, identified by the Loyalty Events Management function, which modify the balance. It can take into account various event types (Product subscription, Telco usage, product purchase, qualitative surveys fulfilled, consumer life - e.g. birthday / subscription date) and transform the events in points or reward value according to business rules (use of a rule engine).
- Burn module: the gained reward value / points represent an accounting value that can be used by the consumer as payment mean, according to the Offer &amp; Product Catalogue rules. The reward can take many forms: rebates / discounts, Telco / no Telco gifts, charity gifts, Telco vouchers, partner vouchers, cash back, etc.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Repository Management Aggregate Function allows to manage the configuration and statuses of the offers and products owned by a customer, according to Offer and Product catalogue descriptions and rules.
- Note: the customer owning the Product might be the CSP when it represents a Product bought by the CSP to a Supplier.

This function also allows:
-  to instantiate or update offers and products ordered by the customer, whatever their type (network product, bundle, device, ) or their marketing mode (rented, sold, ), with their configuration, their tariffs and discounts, and their status (initialized with a creation order)
- to update Offer and Product installed base status
-  to search and read  Product installed base (subscribed offers, configuration of installed products, installed tariffs and discount, statuses, ).
It also includes the check of configuration rules such as prerequisite or mandatory characteristic for each Product submitted.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Loyalty Events Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Loyalty Events Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Loyalty Events Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Loyalty Products are defined in the Product Catalogue, and part of Loyalty Programs that cope with the consumer recognition, the consumer life cycle and promotes the usage development, the attachment to the brand and the discovery of new products. 

The Loyalty Events Management Aggregate Function permits to:

	- collect the events defined by the Loyalty Product (nature and perimeter). Examples : monthly international usage volume, amount of the invoice, customer orders, interactions, customer birthday,
	- evaluate the related Loyalty Rules. Example: if the amount of the invoice is higher than 50,
	- and trigger Loyalty Actions. Example: customer order of 100 SMS free, interaction to notify the customer of a discount, add 100 points on the Loyalty Account.
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Agreement Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Agreement Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Agreement Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Agreement Management Aggregate Function permits to manage agreements related to Product Offerings with Party involved in it and approvals.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Test Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Test Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Test Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Test Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of Product Tests execution and managing the inventory of Product Tests.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Test Execution -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Test Execution">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Test Execution</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Test Execution Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage the end-to-end execution of a test for an atomic product, either for manual or automatic testing, in order to ensure that this atomic product is working properly.

The end-to-end Product Test process will also rely on the Service Test Management and Resource Test Management functions depending on the association between the atomic product and the underlying service or resource.

The Product Test Execution is used by fulfillment and the assurance processes.
In the fulfillment process, the product test management can be triggered to launch acceptance tests to ensure that the product is working properly from the customer/user perspective and thus validate the end of the delivery process. 

On the assurance side the product testing function is used during product problem isolation. 

Product Test Execution functionalities include:

	- Verifying test authorization and quota management: product test execution checks, depending on the initiator of the test request , if it is authorized and if limitation applies in terms of test quota (linked to underlying services/resources limitation or additional marketing constraints.) This applies especially for self-care test from users.
	- Carrying out test priority : the priority of a self-care test for a user is higher than a back-office test.
	- Retrieval of appropriate inventory data and setting up the test context and configuration.
	- Test execution with access to the various data required to perform product testing.

  - A product test main actions will be to trigger service or resource tests depending on the product composition
  - Data collected during product test can be inventory data (for example in IS inventory) or data coming from service or resource tests.
  - Test execution produces a test result that may contain a raw result and an enrichment of this result depending on the product test definition and associated rules.
     For example: for an access product test the raw result can be the associated bandwidth and an enrichment can be done depending on the user access type (fiber, xdsl) to evaluate if the result is suitable or not.

	- Test results restitution: the restitution of the raw test results may depends on the test execution context data.

  - For the same test, depending on the initiator, the result restitution can be more or less detailed. The user/customer needs less technical details in self-care whereas customer care needs more and more details with higher care level.
  - However test result restitution levels must always be mutually consistent through all  the execution context

	- Test results long term logging for offline data analyses. These analyses are done outside of Product Test Management.


	- Possible reporting of test results back to the initiator of the test.
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Operational Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Operational Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Operational Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Operational Analysis Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of conducting on-the-fly analysis, on the collected information concerning offer and product, which may have an influence on the operational activities.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Fraud Detection -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Fraud Detection">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Fraud Detection</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Fraud Detection Aggregate Function consists in the detection of atypical traffics and exceptional consumption and analysis and research of the fraudulent behavior profiles.
It consumes Product Usage Analysis, Service Usage Analysis, Invoice (in case of right diversion distance between the invoice and the valued use) and usage analysis for a specific number (Surcharged number) in front of installed base, invoiced base.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Usage Tracking -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Usage Tracking">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Usage Tracking</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Usage Tracking Aggregate Function is based on data collection processes (network probes, device agent, CDRs, Customer Premise Equipment) and comprises the Follow-up of the product use with an on-the-fly analysis (detailed and aggregated) of the customers uses and comparison with the products analysis forecasts.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Pushed Offer Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Pushed Offer Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Pushed Offer Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Pushed Offer Management Aggregate Function includes:

	- Identifying and pushing offers adapted to the customer needs including personalized offers: based on a customer individualized knowledge (Marketing one to one), the aim is to elaborate for each customer - according to his needs and recent actions - individualized Product Specifications &amp; Offerings.
	- Simulating  Product Specification configuration and Product Offerings on the real traffic of the customer thanks to Tariff Calculation and Rating.

Pushed Offer Management uses and proposes only the offers described in the Commercial and Product Catalogue.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Configuration & Activation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Configuration & Activation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Configuration & Activation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Configuration &amp; Activation is in charge of 
   - configuring Products taking into account constraints between them and applying engineering rules,
   - and activating and pre-activating Products.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Activation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Activation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Activation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Activation Aggregate Function is in charge of activating and pre-activating Products.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Performance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Performance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Performance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Performance Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of assuring that SLA agreements made between operators and customers are met.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Performance Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Performance Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Performance Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Performance Analysis Aggregate Function calculates Product capacity and performance and analysis it.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Performance Reporting -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Performance Reporting">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Performance Reporting</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Performance Reporting Aggregate Function provides special report requested by customer. Some customers may request from their telecommunication operator, some specific reports, for example, concerning their consumption on a particular site, the quality of service on a particular technology. Usually, this kind of service is supported by operators for major corporate customers.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Performance Commitment Follow-up -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Performance Commitment Follow-up">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Performance Commitment Follow-up</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Performance Commitment Follow-up Aggregate Function compares the Quality of Service provided to customers against the SLA Agreement and determines possible customers compensations according to tariff rules associated with the SLA product (service level, recovery time objectives, escalation options). It relies on indicators and measures, of performance and availability provided by the network domain, or related to trouble ticket management (for example trouble processing time). It checks that the Quality of Service meets the required thresholds, provides Quality of Service and performance reports.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Performance Supervision -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Performance Supervision">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Performance Supervision</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Performance Supervision Aggregate Function enables 
- Product functioning to be measured (in real-time or not), 
- and supervised, in order to prevent the product quality and performance degradation, with an on-the-fly analysis. 

It includes: 
- filtering, aggregation of the collected information
- analysis of 
     - different types of information 
     - results of tests or historic of tests
     - product usage
     - traffic degradations. 
- enrich or calculate product quality and performance indicators (includes KQI, KPI) 
     - Examples of KQI: time to repair warranty, availability percentage on a time slot, percentage of correct vocal quality communications
- analysis and evaluation of product performance indicators evolution
- it compares these information and indicators with expected values or pre-established limits
     - when there is a great augmentation of product use and traffic congestion it can trigger a trouble ticket that will be handled via problem management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Problem Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Problem Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Problem Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Problem Management Aggregate Function groups functions responsible for the management of a problem concerning a product.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Trouble Ticket Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Trouble Ticket Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Trouble Ticket Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Trouble Ticket Management Aggregate Function allows to record and monitor failures on product(s), resulting from the qualification of a problem declared by the Customer.
Product Trouble Ticket Management functionalities include:
- Verifying customer - or user - authorization to declare a Product Trouble Ticket
- Completion and validation of the Product Trouble Ticket
- Identification of the internal actor or team in charge of the Product Trouble Ticket management, follow up and reporting to the customer
- Product Trouble Ticket lifecycle management
   Example: it can be necessary to ask customer validation, via Product Test, before closing the Product Trouble Ticket
- Information and possible reporting to the customer during the process
- Escalation procedure.

When a failure noticed on an installed service or resource may impact an installed product - or a set of installed products functioning, Product Trouble Ticket(s) can be initialized to permit personalized information of the concerned customers and to apply SLA conditions.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Trouble Ticket Resolution -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Trouble Ticket Resolution">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Trouble Ticket Resolution</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Trouble Ticket Resolution Aggregate Function allows to qualify a problem declared by the Customer and solve it.
It includes:
- Identification of the installed product(s) concerned by a dysfunction, mostly by taking into account Product Test results,
- Identification of the associated SLA product(s) to identify a priority of treatment or a time limit for the trouble ticket, depending on contractual conditions and financial risks for the operator - and to check if conditions have been enforced or if penalties are due,
- Identification of the installed CFS corresponding to each installed product concerned by the dysfunction (or directly installed resource for goods products),
- Identification of the internal actor or team in charge of the Product Trouble Ticket management, follow up and reporting to the customer,
- Triggering actions to solve the problem including identifying SLA violation and CSR penalties to apply.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Problem Diagnose -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Problem Diagnose">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Problem Diagnose</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Problem Diagnose Aggregate Function identifies the root cause of the Product Problem based on Services and Resources topology, customer's context and resulting of Diagnostics / Testing tools, identifies Services and / or Resources as part of the problem. It is also in charge of managing the Problem Knowledge information and solutions.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Trouble Ticket Orchestration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Trouble Ticket Orchestration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Trouble Ticket Orchestration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Trouble Ticket Orchestration Aggregate Function is used to orchestrate the handling of problems that can be associated with several product malfunctions (Product Trouble Ticket).

It includes: 
- The determination of the installed services (know-how) corresponding to each installed product concerned by the incident (or directly the resources for the tangible products) and the plant in charge of them
- The Identification of the Service Trouble Tickets to be associated with the Product Trouble Ticket(s)
- Identification of the associated SLA product(s) to identify a priority of treatment or a time limit for the trouble ticket, depending on contractual conditions and financial risks for the operator - and to check if conditions have been enforced or if penalties are due
- The Orchestration of links with the service level for the processing of related Trouble Tickets.

Orchestration can take into account:
- constraints between installed products 
- any type of management rule based on information even external to trouble handling. For example: high priority for VIP customers.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Usage Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Usage Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Usage Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Product Usage Management Aggregate Function functions aim at:
- Identify the product(s) concerned by every relevant Service Usage Records (for example CDR) and to produce Product Usage Record(s).
- Filter the Service Usage Records that have no interest for rating or reporting.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Product Balance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Product Balance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Product Balance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Product Balance Management Aggregate Function is responsible for managing any type of balances:
- supervised or not
- for both real-time and batch processing modes of operation
- of any type of unit.
The Balance Management function includes:
- Management of balance(s) related to any bundle: a bundle may have several associated balances, for example a balance representing units previously bought by the customer or granted by the Enterprise, (e.g. airtime bucket, complimentary messaging bucket and s.o.) and their validity periods.
From the data of the product usage record, the function determines if this usage can be assigned to the bundle, if it has to be (for example the user can watch a VOD several times in 48 hours and it is only counted once) and how it has to be (which balance, which calculation rules, )
- Recharging events management: recurrent or one shot, for example with a voucher for Top Up.
- Quota reservation (even in the case of simultaneous sessions management) and refunding in case of non-delivery of the service or partial usage.
When an advice of charge (calculated by the Tariff Calculation and Rating function) is required, before sending the information to the customer, a volume pre-reservation can be performed to guarantee the price for the customer; 
- Transfer of Balance in case of contract migration
Balance management may also have to trigger alarms on usage limits, i.e. when charged usages reach a threshold or an array of thresholds - it shall trigger predefined actions such as SMS, selective barring etc. via dedicated functions such as Interaction Management.
Information handled in Balance Management has to be made available to consumption follow-up.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Rating and Follow up -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Rating and Follow up">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Rating and Follow up</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Rating and Follow Up Aggregate Function groups functions related to rating and to consumption follow up activities.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Bill Calculation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Bill Calculation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Bill Calculation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Bill Calculation Aggregate Function purpose is the preparation of the different items to be billed to the customer by the operator and trading partners (e.g. content providers).

The bill calculation usually collects, and eventually aggregates priced items that have been processed by the rating/pricing functionality and stored until the next bill calculation.
This steps will consist of assembling different kinds of priced items relevant for a same billing account and dedicated period of time - which is concerned by the invoice to produce:
-  recurring fees (as the subscriptions), 
-  non-recurring fees (late payment penalty; product access fees),
-  usage fees,
-  fees coming from other systems or providers.

Complex or global tax rules, or even discounts or penalties, can be applied on these sets of fees. 
For example, VAT is globally applied on the amount of usage fees related to the same classification.

Calculating discounts on volume, implies discounts are calculated when all the elements to be billed have been processed.

Bill calculation may also involve billing on behalf of other providers (bill items coming from external systems).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Tariff Calculation and Rating -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Tariff Calculation and Rating">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Tariff Calculation and Rating</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Tariff Calculation and Rating Aggregate Function aims to identify and apply the relevant pricing rules and discounts, and to calculate the related price,
-   to any type of tariff (recurring, one-time , or related to product usage records)
- during Sales, Order Capture and Order follow-up processes to produce an installedTariff or a quotation or a simulation, or to complete a customer order or update an installedOffer,
-   as well as during the Usage Rating process (ex: for a product usage record).
For example, some products usages require an advice of charge, i.e. the customer has to be presented with a price estimate (quotation) before using the product. In this case, the real-time rating module calculates the price depending on the offers and products owned by the customer, and delivers the information to the customer during the authorization request for the usage.

This function evaluates the pricing rules and discount defined in the commercial catalogue for the corresponding offer and tariff type, identifies and applies relevant one(s) to calculate unitary prices (without and with VAT). It can also take into account Framework Agreement special conditions. 

For each applied tariff, the function identifies if an immediate payment is required, if loyalty burn is possible, or if a billing account has to be associated, based on rules declared in the commercial catalogue.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Strategic Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Strategic Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Strategic Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Strategic Analysis Aggregate Function groups functions useful to conduct delayed analysis, on the collected information concerning market and customer, which may have an influence on the strategy.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Experience Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Experience Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Experience Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Experience Analysis Aggregate Function is in charge of analyzing customers experiences based on Customer behavior information.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Loyalty and Retention Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Loyalty and Retention Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Loyalty and Retention Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Loyalty and Retention Analysis function undertake trend analysis on loyalty and retention, including customer acquisitions and churn.
It comprises:
- Follow-up of the churn rate: Development of a report in order to measure by period (periodicity: weekly, monthly, yearly),
- The number and rate of terminations by offer and product,
- The trend of the churn rate according to the previous periods,
- Prediction of the churn (anticipate in order to minimize the attrition): identification of the potential churner profiles from a predictive model analyzing the churner profiles according to the previous periods.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Behavior Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Behavior Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Behavior Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Behavior Analysis Aggregate Function concerns the follow-up &amp; Analysis of the customers behavior.
It includes:
- Analysis of the contacts by email, SMS, phone, mail and Web (Web use, chat), social network use (facebook, twitter, and so on),
- Analysis of the customers contacts in order to determine the channel effectiveness,
- Analysis of the content effectiveness from measures concerning the consultations, web page navigation, web click-stream on our portals and mobile applications, purchase, acts registered
- Analysis of the customers behavior with predictive analysis such as scoring, segmentations, profiling to analyze the users behavior in order to improve targeting and segmentation or to identify next best actions to promote our products, e-reputation or brand.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Information Specification Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Information Specification Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Information Specification Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Information Specification Management Aggregate Function is in charge of specifying information and its calculation rules for Customer Information that needs to be calculated.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Information Specification Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Information Specification Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Information Specification Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Information Specification Development Aggregate Function is in charge of

	- defining customer information to be collected,
	- designing Customer profile specification, including rules to be applied for each threshold in order to determine the customer value from its uses (traffic, products and services used), and corresponding revenues and costs.
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Behavior Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Behavior Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Behavior Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Behavior Development Aggregate Function is in charge of defining actions to be triggered according to the Customer behavior such as preventions actions according to his usages.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Billing Definition -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Billing Definition">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Billing Definition</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Billing Definition Aggregate Function manages the data that are necessary to pilot invoice production: billing cycle data, management of runs, groups and cycles of invoicing.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Risk Mitigation Definition -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Risk Mitigation Definition">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Risk Mitigation Definition</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Risk Mitigation Definition Aggregate Function is meant to define an array of actions, related to triggering conditions such as threshold(s) of billable amounts defined according to risk level, to avoid bill shock.
It also defines Customer criteria (such as VIP) to identify the corresponding prevention scenario to be applied.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Dunning Scenario Definition -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Dunning Scenario Definition">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Dunning Scenario Definition</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">TBA</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Loyalty Program Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Loyalty Program Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Loyalty Program Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Loyalty Program Management AF includes functions that define and oversee the creation of, operation of, and evolution of customer loyalty programs.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Information Support -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Information Support">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Information Support</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Information Support Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of collecting, qualifying and managing Customer information and also personalizing Customer profile.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Collect and Qualify Customer Information -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Collect and Qualify Customer Information">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Collect and Qualify Customer Information</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Collect and Qualify Customer Information Aggregate Function concerns the management of our knowledge of the individual customer to keep or produce an up-to-date, accurate and legally compliant Customer information. 

It will incorporate into the customer profile, all relevant information gathered through all contacts with the customer (demographics, life stage, household, community of interest, business direction) and customer experience offered (quality of service experienced, customer inquiries).
Customer and market information from other sources may be gathered, which will build a better understanding of the customer.

Customer Information must be made available to any process that needs to access it. 
This customer information will be used to continually refine the means and style of interaction, and the solution sets.

Knowledge and consent of the party may be required to store any collected information and to use it (example: opt-in).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Information Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Information Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Information Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Information Management Aggregate Function enables the creation and updates of customer information such as name, address(es) and site(s) location, organisation, contact person and contact person roles (legal representative, subscriber, payer, end-user, fleet manager) and contact methods.
The term Customer is used in the broad sense: it represents here person or organisation, potential, existing or former customer.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Personalize Customer Interaction -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Personalize Customer Interaction">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Personalize Customer Interaction</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Personalize Customer Interaction Aggregate Function concerns the definition of scripts to make commercial dialog easier and more efficient between customers (including users or prospects) and Customer Service Representative (CSR) or Interactive Voice Response (IVR), 
These scripts can be used during any type of interaction, either to make qualification or sales as well as polls or assistance.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Behavior Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Behavior Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Behavior Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Behavior Management Aggregate Function all information related to the customer behavior and actions to be triggered accordingly.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Behavior Tracking -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Behavior Tracking">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Behavior Tracking</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Behavior Tracking function concerns the necessary functionality to monitor the Customer Behavior during its relationships with the CSP, public communications and its products uses.
It includes:

	- Track of the contacts by email, SMS, phone, mail and Web (Web use, chat) to provide a detailed and precise understanding of the customer behavior in front of the Telco operator solicitations (marketing campaigns, customer inquiries) or during a customer request.
	- Track all user steps on the web site and influence on social networks: click-stream, viewed pages, consulted product descriptions, comments. In real time, this function analyses the user behavior, identifies his centers of interest and then deduces his profile which will be used to push contents and offers adequate for the user / customer needs.
	- Track of the customer behavior in order to provide a global and personalized vision of its real consumption on all its products based on a detailed view of each product consumption.
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Order Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Order Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Order Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Management functions manage the end to end lifecycle of a Customer Order request from the establishment to the complete delivery. This includes Customer Order Establishment, Customer Order Tracking and LifeCycle Management, as well as Customer Order Orchestration.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Order Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Order Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Order Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Repository Management Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to the Customer Order and to its items (e.g. : Order lines).
When the Customer Order is completed, this function publishes the event Order Completed. This event usually triggers the Customer Order Orchestration function to deliver the Customer Order.
It gathers product items delivery events from Customer Order Orchestration and manages related Customer Order lifecycle. It also manages these statuses in case of Order cancellation.
Depending on the Customer Order (or any of its elements) events, and on the implemented business rules, this function can decide to notify automatically the customer of the Customer Order improvement. The notification itself can be made by SMS / email / other medium and is done by the functions of the Welcome and Interaction Aggregate Function.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Offer and Product Configuration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Offer and Product Configuration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Offer and Product Configuration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Offer and Product Configuration function enables the configuration of the commercial offer chosen by the customer (period of engagement for example) and the products it markets (for examples: telephone number, quantity of SMS/MMS, hours of voice calls, ...). 
The configuration covers the choice of optional offers and products, the choice of the functions values, etc.
It can be based on product configurator using a rule engine.
It can be used at the same time by Selling, Order Establishment or Develop Sales Proposal Business activities.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Order Eligibility Validation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Order Eligibility Validation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Order Eligibility Validation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Order Commercial Eligibility Aggregate Function validates that the Offer &amp; products specified on the Customer Order, are available and feasible from a commercial and functional point of view.
It includes:
- Commercial Eligibility with commercial compatibility with the already customer installed Offers and with customer profile (age, location, ) 
- Functional Eligibility with functional compatibility with the already customer installed Products.
- Customer Credit Eligibility check, considering the payment history and the credit scores for a Customer, only the predictive credit score for a Prospective Customer.
The technical feasibility at the product level is verified relying on the Service Availability function.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Order Quotation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Order Quotation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Order Quotation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Order Quotation Aggregate Function enables the elaboration of an estimate, including configuration, pricing and feasibility elements, thanks to the specific configuration, eligibility and valuation functions, identifying the various relevant interlocutors (through the Sales Interlocutors Management function), the scenarios to be realized and the lot.
The quotation can be materialized via a document and/or associated to a simplified identification mean (QR code, bar code, ) to ease later interactions and identification of the quotation, and sent to the customer.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Order Completion -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Order Completion">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Order Completion</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Order Completion function enables to complete and finalize the Customer Order, after its validation with the requester and before triggering its delivery, with collect of Customer Data.
The complements could concern customer order links with actors, dates, appointment, address, billing account.

The final validation of the Customer Order can trigger the generation of documents such as:
- a contract document, summing up the customer order, and the standard and specific sales terms
- a simplified identification mean such as QR / bar code, and its sending to the customer, by MMS, email,  to ease later interactions and identification of the order. Example: to pick up in store a goods product ordered from web portal or mobile apps, the customer just needs to present the sent QR / bar code.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Order Change Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Order Change Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Order Change Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Change Management Aggregate Function amends pending order resulted from customer change requests or provisioning system limitation and revalidate the order.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Order Validation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Order Validation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Order Validation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Order Validation Aggregate Function permits to validate the Customer Order, or the cancellation of a Customer Order, with the requester, according to Offer and Product catalogue and installed base (see Customer Order Eligibility function), and to provisioning rules and delays, after tariff calculation with possibly Framework agreement special conditions (see Tariff calculation and rating function).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Order Orchestration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Order Orchestration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Order Orchestration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Order Orchestration Aggregate Function provides workflow and orchestration capabilities at the Product Order Item level to deliver a dedicated Customer Order. 

Orchestration can take into account :
- Constraints between Product Order Items inside the Customer Order, or successive Customer Orders including modification or cancellation (in-flight changes)
- Any type of business rules based on information even external to the Customer Order. For example : high level of priority for VIP customers
- Triggering of exception process or delivery planning update, depending on Customer Order or Service Order events.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Order Delivery Preparation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Order Delivery Preparation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Order Delivery Preparation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Order Delivery Preparation Aggregate Function allows in the context of a customer order to:
- query catalogues and installed bases to identify what needs to be delivered: Service Specification (CFS SPec) and its configuration, Stock Item, Supplier Product, Work Spec, related to the ordered product,
- prepare a Service Order (CFS Order), Supplier Order, Stock Item Order or Work Order with the necessary information.
In the case of a Product associated with an Internal Service (Know-How), this function also allows to: 
- check if a corresponding Installed CFS is operational in the Service Installed Base, and so determine the operation at CFS level (creation or modification)
- possibly group in the same Service Order several ordered products, based on the same CFS specification, and/or identified to be delivered at the same time by the Customer Order Delivery Orchestration.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Voucher Activation Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Voucher Activation Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Voucher Activation Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The (material or electronic) voucher materializes for example a mobile top-up, a refund, a promotional code. 
The Voucher Activation Aggregate Function performs voucher activation management, after it was bought or ordered and paid.
It checks validity and authenticity of voucher, and marks it as used once it has been completed.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Problem Support -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Problem Support">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Problem Support</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Problem Support Aggregate Function contains functions responsible for the management of customer request concerning problem, assistance or information.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Assistance -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Assistance">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Assistance</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Assistance Aggregate Function permits to bring a help to qualify a dysfunction indicated by a user or customer and to suggest basic solution to restore a properly functioning. 
This can be built from list of questions (script), previous experiences, knowledge bases and best-practice descriptions, chat with a CSR,  according to the dysfunction typology. 
It may comprise:
- identification of the customer - or user - and verifying authorization to access assistance via Person Identification and Authentication function
- information on how to use a product, best practices
- check actions like reboot (to see if the dysfunction continues), check of the subscription, configuration check, 
- identification of the product(s) type(s) concerned by a dysfunction
- information concerning collective trouble which may impact the customer or user
   When further to a collective trouble, a message of information is identified by the Service Impact Assessment function, the Customer Assistance function is in charge of implementing this communication (on various support like chat, web, Interactive Voice Server).

If during this phase, the dysfunction is confirmed, the Assurance Process will continue with the Product Trouble Ticket Management and/or Product Test function.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Case Workflow Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Case Workflow Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Case Workflow Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Case Workflow Management Aggregate Function is in charge of identifying steps of the case, sequencing them and orchestrating the Case Workflow.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Financial Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Financial Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Financial Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Financial Management Aggregate Function groups functions which manage the processing of financial transactions affecting the customer's financial account.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Bill Dispute -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Bill Dispute">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Bill Dispute</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Bill Dispute Aggregate Function consists of handling the customer inquiries received through customer service, self-care or any other channel, and for all type of product, about their bills that have not been solved by simple actions in the Welcome process (e.g. walk customer through their invoice).
- The initial customer contact and the disputed amount are recorded.
- The case is investigated using billing data (invoice images, billed items, usage history) A/R data (e.g. search of unmatched payment) and others (e.g. installed products, network databases).
- The dispute settlement results in customer notification of the results, dispute granted (for the whole disputed amount or part of it) or customer request denied.
- The case is closed.
When the dispute is granted, the granted amount must be taken off the debt, through a debit adjustment. Depending on business rules in force, this may be billed either as part of the following periodic invoice, or as a specific invoicing process and related accounting document (in this case a credit note).
The reason for the adjustment must be recorded, for example adjustment may be a gesture of goodwill from the company, or a correction for a proven billing error. Depending on local law and regulation, the process and applicable rules may be different.
In addition, if the investigation showed an error in product and services data (e.g. wrong service affected to a contract), the settlement will also include an update request to the installed offers and products repository.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Payment Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Payment Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Payment Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Payment Management function is aimed at processing all payment flows issued or received by the Enterprise:
- Regardless of the payment method (address all payment methods supported by the Enterprise), for example a cash payment or a payment with loyalty points to buy an accessory, a payment on invoice to pay the subscription, a payment via a scheduled plan to buy a device
- For prepaid as well as post-paid offers.
- Immediate payments are also recorded (for instance for offer including equipment or terminals sales)
- it also includes management of deposits related to orders.

It comprises the main following sub-functions.
Payment flow management which role is to:
- Manage all of the relations with the banking sector (unique portal), both outgoing (direct debit requests, payments by credit card, refunds by cheque or by bank transfer, etc.) and incoming (for example for bank transfers and cheques files, payment rejections, etc.) for customers,
- Manage all flows related to banking (supervision, management of bank opening schedules) and allow a rapid response in the case of problems.
It includes:
- Formatting of the orders: to the 3rd party systems for outgoing payments (depending on the Electronic Funds Transfer (EFT) agreements with banks or other 3rd parties: e.g. Electronic Data Interchange (EDI) message) and to the originating systems for the incoming payments,
- Grouping and ordering of orders (e. g. group payment requests by target company and source company).

It handles both outgoing (mostly payment requests, occasional refunds), and incoming events (payments or rejections).
- Payment management
This functionality handles the business logic needed to perform a payment request that can require on-line interface with the banking system. It addresses use cases such as :
1. Payment request to top-up prepaid balance / account
A payer wishes to pay on-line for a prepaid account top-up (payer may be the account holder or not),
2. Direct Payment related to a customer order (on-line or in shops) : sale of a device, or deposit for a rented device
3. Payment request to settle a debt.
A payer wishes to pay for one invoice (main case), or a subset of an invoice (partial payment).
- Payment tractability and banking reconciliation
Generically, the payment functionality should involve tracing and querying capabilities that allow to trace back incoming payments to the originating event (outgoing payment request or invoice). A payment can be partial.
- Payment recycling / re-allocation
Depending on the implementation of interfaces with banking or other 3rd party systems, it may happen that incoming payments are received with ambiguous data, i.e. cannot be traced back with certainty to an originating event. In this case, a recycling functionality is needed.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Dunning -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Dunning">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Dunning</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Dunning Aggregate Function allows the different steps of the dunning to be managed until its resolution (payment or write off).
It covers soft and hard dunning phases (soft dunning ends with contract termination).
It comprises the following sub-functions:
- Update financial scoring: update of the customer scoring information, based on events that are significant of payer behavior of the customer (e.g. late payment, delays, etc.) 
- Initiate dunning strategy: implementation of the companys dunning strategy within its information system. 
Example: A payment schedule is assigned according to a variety of customer criteria, such as customer type, payment means, payment behavior, etc.
- Manage the dunning and event process: Management of the dunning process pertaining to a dossier and the automatic or manual chaining of actions, as issuing a SMS, a letter, an outbound call, a barring action (suspension, restriction or even contract termination, via an order) or delay fees. 
- Business and follow-up activities: Control and management of the dunning activity. It generates statistics.
- Penalty Charges: Automatic and manual generation of the penalty charges (dunning charges, payment rejection fees) linked to dunning of the due amount, in order to bill the customer according to the details of his / her contract..
- Payment delay agreement and promises: Inclusion of a payment schedule or a promise of payment, negotiated with the customer during the dunning process. 
- Write off: When the debt is considered as irrecoverable, the amount of the debt is written off (the dunning file is closed and the customer subledger accounting is informed).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Loan Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Loan Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Loan Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Loan Management Aggregate Function includes functions involved in managing the lifecycle and administration of Loans, as viewed from the perspective of the lender.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Invoice Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Invoice Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Invoice Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Invoice Management Aggregated Function groups functions related to the production of the customer invoices and to prevention activities.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Billing Account Administration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Billing Account Administration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Billing Account Administration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Billing Account administration Aggregate Function manages all the information related to the billing account, needed to produce an invoice and agreed with the customer such as :
- The associated invoicing group (linked to the periodicity and the date of the invoice production),
- Perimeter of installed contracts and products charged to the billing account, and for which any type of charges can be collected (one time charges, recurring charges or usage charges),
- Parties and party roles concerned by the billing process, such as Holder, Payer, Bill receiver, etc.,
- Invoice structure and presentation rules,
- And possibly default payment means for automatic generation of payments.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Invoicing -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Invoicing">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Invoicing</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Invoicing Aggregate Function comprises two steps: bill composition (i.e. sorting and assembly of bill items) and bill production. 
It enables either physical or electronic invoices, through the formatting and generation of final form (paper, CD-ROM, pdf ) and delivery (sending by e-mail, post or displaying through a web self-care portal). 
On electronic format, the invoice can include tools enabling consumption analysis for corporate needs for instance.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Risk Mitigation Measures Enforcement -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Risk Mitigation Measures Enforcement">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Risk Mitigation Measures Enforcement</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Risk Mitigation Measures Enforcement Aggregate Function enforces the Risk Mitigation  scenarios previously defined by the Customer Risk Mitigation Definition, that is:
- Detection of billable amounts threshold (according to billing account owners risk profile and related scenario),
- Triggering of actions (e.g.: sending alert SMS, triggering product barring) via dedicated functions.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Loyalty and Retention -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Loyalty and Retention">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Loyalty and Retention</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Loyalty and Retention Aggregate Function groups functions related to the use of loyalty schemes to attract and gain the loyalty of customers, and to the retention of acquired customers.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Risk Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Risk Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Risk Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Risk Management Aggregate Function is based on information collected from all internal processes and that consistent risk assessment is used across the Enterprise Detection to manage with customer axes, fraud risk, influence risk, and churn risk.
It includes also detection of the outstanding payment risks, for a new customer or during a new order for example, with:
- Definition of the class "bad debtors, from analysis of the profiles of bad debtor behaviors.
- Score calculation from collected information related to the customer</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Personalize Customer Profile -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Personalize Customer Profile">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Personalize Customer Profile</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Personalize Customer Profile Aggregate Function includes:

	- Steering of the commercial pressure: manages the number of contacts with a customer in order to solicit him only if appropriate. The commercial pressure concerns the under and over customer solicitation.
	- Personalization of the customer relationship: based on a follow-up of the customer behavior, the aim is to select some key elements in order to personalize the relation during the customer contacts (contacts with the Telco operator services initiated by the customer).
	- Identification of the interaction channels by customer or segment: Identification of the optimal interaction channel for each customer or customer segment, based on the profile study and the history of the contacts.
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Value Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Value Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Value Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Value Management Aggregate Function comprises:

	- Measure and follow up of the customer value: Development of a model in order to determine the customer value from its uses (traffic, products and services used), and corresponding revenues and costs. The follow-up of the customer value evolution (identification of the evolution tendencies of the value and actions concerning the creation or destruction of value, Average Revenue Per User, margin per user).
	- Predictive Scoring of the new customer behavior: using a predictive model based on the histories of customers having the similar profile, determine the expected evolution of the customer value and his lifetime. Customer scoring is performed when customers sign up a contract, then enriched and updated by events occurring during the customer lifecycle (e.g. revenue generated, usage patterns, payment behavior...). The output of customer scoring could be a risk level or an opportunity.
	- Segmentation based on the customer value and/or lifetime value: Regrouping of the customers in relevant sets (even subsets) for Marketing. The segmentation criteria use the customer value, the customer lifetime value, the product and service uses (consumption behaviors).
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Welcome and Interaction -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Welcome and Interaction">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Welcome and Interaction</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Welcome and Interaction Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of managing all interfaces between the company and the existing or prospective customer.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Interaction Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Interaction Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Interaction Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Customer Interaction Management Aggregate Function manages all type of interactions with Customers (e.g. customer, prospect, product user, ) and enables recording such interactions (duration, type and reason), manages interactions follow-up, escalation and historical background.

An interaction can be initiated:
- by a Customer (inbound contact), through the Welcome process, to identify his needs, problems, 
- by the CSP or one of its representative (outbound contact), through the Campaign Execution, Welcome, Order Follow up,  processes, 
     - to inform the customer about our offers, promotions, 
     - or about his on-going requests, queue position, 
     - or to alert him in case of network problem, over consumption, payment delay, </rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Context Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Context Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Context Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Context Management Aggregate Function allows to define the needed information and the first actions to be initiated once the person has been identified and authenticated. Moreover, information and actions should be set in accordance with the channel used by the actors (human, system).
Different rules have to be set in accordance with:
- Context of the request
- Role of the person initiating the request
- Customer contract situation
- List of possible actions with priorities
- The CSR or seller role and expertise level, if present, in the context of the request
- Presentation rules for the channel or device involved.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Queue Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Queue Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Queue Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Queue Management Aggregate Function permits to manage queues when customers need to interact with the CSP customer service representatives (CSR), whatever the channel:

	- in shops, the customer can go to the reception desk, be identified via NFC or can self-register via automatic desk
	- by phone, via the customer phone number, the customer identifier, ...
	- on web portal, via the customer credentials


When an interaction is initialized and if the CSR can't be immediately identified or is not available, the Queue Management Aggregate Function permits to associate the interaction to the relevant team - or team member - queue, depending on the type of request.
It can also manage

	- priority assignment, depending on the customer (ex: premium customer) or on type of request
	- waiting time calculation - and customer information
	- possibility to make an appointment


When the CSR is available, this function can trigger a SMS push, for example, to inform the customer (via the Interaction Management function).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Customer Request Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Customer Request Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Customer Request Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Request Management Aggregate Functionallows:
1) The capture of all information required by the Customer Request according to its type or nature. Depending on the type or nature of the Customer Request, the list of information to capture may strongly vary. Depending also on information captured, additional information may be required. The Customer Request information capture shall use configuration parameters and rules to determine which information needs to be captured and when.
2) The qualification of the Customer Request according to configured criteria and information captured. Customer Request Management shall be able to determine the result of the captured Customer Request. This result may either be: the triggering of the Sales subdomain functions (from Offer and Product Development, Management and Operation Domain), the generation of a Customer Problem (that may correspond to a Bill Dispute), the generation of a Product Trouble Ticket.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Strategy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Strategy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Strategy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Strategy Management Aggregate Function groups functions useful to define the service strategy and to develop it (new services or enhance the existing services).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Strategy Design and Planning -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Strategy Design and Planning">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Strategy Design and Planning</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Strategy, Design and Planning Aggregate Function is responsible for the definition of the service strategy at the company level. This function takes into account a list of inputs to determine the need to implement new services, to modify or replace existing services or to stop them. As inputs, the function can use the following information:
- The existing context of the company with available services and resources (network, PFS)
- The data produced by the Service Usage Analysis function
- The performance, quality and test results of existing services or in test services (like POC to test new services in run)
- The new services and underlying technologies that are developed in the industry (following services trend, new generation of network, competitors...) and evaluation of the appeal for these new solutions including information provided by the Offer and Product Strategy function
- Marketing constraints concerning the date of opening for new service
- The cost of the existing and new services and technologies with benefit evaluation
- The possibility or the obligation to provide the services to third parties
- The resource strategy defined in a bottom up process (techno-push)

Following the definition of the service strategy, and after the definition of the resource strategy and the resource design, the Service Strategy, Design and Planning function updates the service design by requesting the modifications (creating, updating, deleting) of the service specifications to the service catalogue and thus the evolution of the know-how.

Finally, the Service Strategy, Design and Planning function provides a high level planning of the strategy implementation. This high level plan contains for example the choice of technology with selected options (VHBB with FTTH or RFOG or both), the site management strategy, the selection of partners to provide contents The strategy planning can take into account constraints (like legal constraints or the need of a validation by authorities) and notify other companies, local authorities or governments of the chosen strategy.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Strategic Analysis Aggregate Function groups functions useful to conduct delayed analysis, on the collected information concerning service, which may have an influence on the strategy.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Diagnostic and Test Relevance Quality Loop -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Diagnostic and Test Relevance Quality Loop">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Diagnostic and Test Relevance Quality Loop</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Diagnostic and Test Relevance Quality Loop Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage a quality loop on diagnostic to continually tweak and improve it.
Functionalities include:

	- Management of success/failure result feedback of diagnostic and correction provided by technicians or the systems after a correction
	- Offline analysis of the success/failure data compared to the diagnostic and correction rules to provide modifications to improve them. This analysis is done using statistical diagnostic data and test results to evaluate their relevance and the needed updates.

The provided improvements can be used to update the definition of diagnostic rules and correction actions in the Service Problem Policy Management function and the definition of tests in the Service Test Policy Management function.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Usage Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Usage Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Usage Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Usage Analysis Aggregate Function comprises analysis of the Service use and traffic and calculates Service Usage forecast between operators according to Service Usage Analysis.

It includes forecasts of traffic between operators in order to:
- allow traffic visualization according to various criteria (produced, country, carriers, services, routings)
- analyze the traffic measured, developed and billed with KPIs such as volume in minutes, number of communications, number of messages, amount valued / charged expressed by nature by call, by products, countries, cities or regions, by operators, by network type (2G, 3G, 4G), by contracts on the market international Wholesale in purchase / sales.
- deliver mobile KPIs such as the number of unique Roaming users per product (Month To Date / Year To Date)
- deliver analysis to optimize the network elements involved.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Capability Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Capability Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Capability Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Capability Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of specifying and managing Service Specification capabilities.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Test Resource Check -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Test Resource Check">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Test Resource Check</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Resource Check Aggregate Function is in charge of defining, implementing and checking resources used to test Services.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Deployment and Retirement -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Deployment and Retirement">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Deployment and Retirement</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Deployment and Retirement Aggregate Function is responsible for implementing the service strategy high level plan and develop the service infrastructure that will be used to provide the service (through products) to the customers at large (mass market, enterprise, to third parties/partners...) The Service Deployment and Retirement function manages the deployment constraints like area limitation (for test or for marketing purposes), progressive deployment following precise milestones

The service deployment and retirement can be responsible to manage constraints (like legal constraints or the need of a validation by authorities) and notify other companies, local authorities or governments of the effective deployment or retirement of a service.

Based on the resources developed in the Resource Installed Base, the Service Installed Base is updated and pre-configured if needed, for example for generic services with no specific customer configuration.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Quality and Performance Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Quality and Performance Management Aggregate Function groups functions that supervise service functioning and manage resulting alarms.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Performance Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Performance Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Performance Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Performance Development Aggregate Function enables to define the strategy and policy concerning supervision at service level, that is to say the indicators to calculate, to measure service performance and quality, and the conditions of alarm activation.
It includes:
- definition of indicators associated to the service types (declension of the pattern metrics at the service level), with thresholds, calculation rules and consequences
     - example: rate of not cut communications (=maintained 2 minutes), availability indicator
- definition of the alarm setting off rules 
- definition of severity and risk level evaluation rules 
     - internal rule with regard to the area of the impact
     - rule linked to the contract (SLA)
          - bringing constraints on the interruption slot or requiring to plan the interruption with the customer in the case of scheduled works.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Quality Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Quality Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Quality Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Quality Development Aggregate Function provides establishment of Service quality indicators specifications and the rules connected to them.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Performance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Performance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Performance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Performance Management aggregate function groups functions that both proactively and reactively optimize the delivery of services to customers.  This can include a real-time, end-to-end view to ensure that each service is functioning correctly as well as a historical view. These functions build on the Resource Performance data and active end-end service performance test data to provide a view of a service.They provide a key input to determine the Quality of Service.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Specification Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Specification Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Specification Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Specification Development Aggregate Function enable to create and update the catalog at Service level :
- definition of the Service Providers know-hows (Customer Facing Services specification) and pre-requisite
- definition of the technical solutions available for each know-how (Resource Facing Services specification)
- links with the Supplier Product Catalog for know-hows, technical solutions or part of technical solutions not realized by the Service Provider itself.
It is also responsible for implementing the service strategy high level plan and develop the service infrastructure that will be used to provide the service (through products) to the customers at large (mass market, enterprise, to third parties/partners...) The Service Deployment and Retirement function manages the deployment constraints like area limitation (for test or for marketing purposes), progressive deployment following precise milestones</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Technical Solution Policy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Technical Solution Policy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Technical Solution Policy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Technical Solution Policy Management Aggregate Function enables to define - and to check consistency of - potentially complex rules to automate the choice of the technical solution for a know-how - or parts of the technical solution.

Criteria can be of various nature and combined :
- geographical
- depending on the chosen characteristics values of the know-how
- depending on other products / know-hows of the customer - installed or ordered
- depending on the current state of Service Platforms, Network equipment (occupation rate, load balancing,  )
- depending on Supplier/Partner contracts (volume discounts, )</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Specification Design -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Specification Design">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Specification Design</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Specification Design Aggregate Function enables to create and update the catalogue at Service level :
- definition of the Service Providers know-hows (CustomerFacingServices specification) and pre-requisite
- definition of the technical solutions available for each know-how (ResourceFacingServicesspecification)
- links with the Supplier Product Catalogue for know-hows, technical solutions or part of technical solutions not realized by the Service Provider itself.

These know-hows specifications (and their technical solutions specifications) can then be used to define Products specifications which will be ordered by customers and delivered, but also for the internal needs of the Service Provider to manage its Service Platforms and network infrastructure process.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Test Specification Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Test Specification Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Test Specification Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Specification Development Aggregate Function functions provide the necessary functionalities to manage the service test catalog containing the service test specifications, the rules that define the test configuration setting up based on the context, the rules that define the strategies for executing the test as well as how the test results should be restituted.
Test strategies can range from simple to complex and encompass the definition of test scenario.
The definition of the test (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the question that is asked and thus shapes the result that is reported back.
The functionalities include providing:
- Service Test Specification and test scenario management
  - Test scenario management: for example, a home equipment self-test can trigger a test scenario that verifies the functioning of all the services associated, depending on the installed products of the user. Thus, the Box could test its internal configuration and functioning and its capacity to connect to internet and VoIP services.
  - Managing service test capabilities: some resources used to implement services have limited capabilities for executing tests, especially for multiple parallel testing. All the constraints linked to limited resources test capabilities and depending on the execution context must be aggregated and managed at service test level.
  - Managing initiator authorization: some tests are restricted to some user roles (internal or external) so the authorization are defined at the Service Test Strategy and Policy Management
- Test rules defining the strategies for setting up test configuration
- Test rules defining how to carrying out the service test
  - For example: to choose the next test in a scenario depending on the results of the previous tests
- Test rules defining the enrichment of test results. 
  - For example: enrichment of internet bandwidth measure based on installed product
- Test rules defining the restitution of the result depending on test execution context
   - For example: a test result of ok/not ok can be enough for a user whereas customer care requires more details in the result of the test.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Test Policy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Test Policy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Test Policy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Policy Management Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage the service test catalogue containing the service test specifications, the rules that define the test configuration setting up based on the context, the rules that define the strategies for executing the test as well as how the test results should be restituted.
Test execution strategies can range from simple to complex and encompass the definition of test scenario.
The definition of the test (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the question that is asked and thus shapes the result that is reported back.
The functionalities include providing:
- Service Test Specification and test scenario management
  - Test scenario management: for example, a home equipment self-test can trigger a test scenario that verifies the functioning of all the services associated, depending on the installed products of the user. Thus, the box could test its internal configuration and functioning and its capacity to connect to internet and VoIP services.
  - Managing service test capabilities: some resources used to implement services have limited capabilities for executing tests, especially for multiple parallel testing. All the constraints linked to limited resources test capabilities and depending on the execution context must be aggregated and managed at service test level.
  - Managing initiator authorization: some tests are restricted to some user roles (internal or external) so the authorization are defined at the Service Test Strategy and Policy Management
- Test rules defining the strategies for setting up test configuration
- Test rules defining how to carrying out the service test
  - For example: to choose the next test in a scenario depending on the results of the previous tests
- Test rules defining the enrichment of test results. 
  - For example: enrichment of internet bandwidth measure based on installed product
- Test rules defining the restitution of the result depending on test execution context
   - For example: a test result of ok/not ok can be enough for a user whereas customer care requires more details in the result of the test.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Management groups functions in charge of managing the inventory of installed services.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Repository Management Aggregate Function stores and maintains the instances of know-hows (CustomerFacingServices) corresponding to products, ordered by customers, their configuration and links between them (ex: relies on).

It also describes and maintains, for each installed know-how (or service), the links with the chosen technical solution (ResourceFacingServiceSpec and corresponding Resources) which permit to manage the change of a technical solution - or part of it - for instances of know-how (example: new generation of Service Platform to deploy).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Impact Assessment -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Impact Assessment">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Impact Assessment</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Impact Assessment Aggregate Function enables the calculation of the impact of a resource event or set of events, depending on severity and risk level evaluation rules defined by the Service Performance Developement function.

The resource event can be: 
- a resource trouble or a resource trouble ticket
- an alarm on the resource
- a work planned on the resource (ResourceOrder issued from a Delivery process, from infrastructure operations, from problem management ) or a Project concerning the resource
The nature and the estimated duration of the event can be used for the impact analysis. 

Different types of evaluation rules can be defined, for example:
- rules based on the volume of the impact (example: number of installed services using an installed resource)
- rules based on the related service level agreement (example : a unique installed service can be impacted but with a very high SLA) 

This function provides functionality to:
- identify impacted elements: makes the link between the impacted installed resource and directly or indirectly impacted installed services and installed products (example: an access trouble impacts services supported by the access)
- determine the level, severity and the risk level of the impact,
- helps to plan the execution in appropriate time slot, when customer agreement is required and/or to comply with SLA
- make sure that the means to process the impact are correctly identified, affected and overseen.
- get back the necessary information to be able to notify the concerned customers.

In an assurance context, this function consumes information such as Installed Resource and Service (including SLA), ResourceTrouble, ResourceTroubleTicket, ResourceAlarm. In an infrastructure operation context, this function consumes information such as ResourceOrder, ProjectElement.
It can produce indicators - such as severity and risk indicator - or identify constraints or choice element concerning the intervention slot in order to minimize impacts.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Audit -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Audit">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Audit</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Audit Aggregate Function permits to identify and check the consistency of the current state and the configured characteristics of the installed Services, between the Service Repository, Service Platforms and Network Equipment. Its to ensure that ordered and delivered Products / Know-hows are well configured and activated. 
This function can be used after changes of equipment or software for example.

Several mechanisms are available such as :
- Notification 
- Polling - can be automatic or on-demand (in case of Trouble Ticket for example).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Reconciliation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Reconciliation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Reconciliation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Reconciliation Aggregate Function defines, suggests to an administrator or directly applies, rules to treat gaps between the installed Service Repository and the current state of installed Services on Service Platforms or equipment, identified by the Service Audit. 
These rules describe in which cases it is necessary to update the Service Repository, via the Service Repository Management, or to update the Service Platform itself (ex: Mobile Line and Voice Mail) through specific operations comparable to delivery ones.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Test Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Test Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Test Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Management Aggregate Function functions provide the necessary functionalities to manage the end-to-end execution of a test for a service (test of a service use case getting through all concerned resources, local loop, DSLAM, Service Platform, etc.), either for manual or automatic testing in order to ensure that this service is working properly.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Test Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Test Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Test Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Analysis Aggregate Function analysis Service Test results in order to propose actions to solve issues and provide reporting.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Test Conducting -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Test Conducting">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Test Conducting</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Conducting Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage the end-to-end execution of a test for a service (test of a service use case getting through all concerned resources, local loop, DSLAM, Service Platform, ), either for manual or automatic testing in order to ensure that this service is working properly.
This function is used in the following processes:
- fulfillment for ensuring that the assigned service works as designed by testing the end-to-end service implementation. 
- problem management which can trigger automatic tests during service problem isolation. 
- service supervision, performance &amp; quality management to acquire data necessary to analyze the running services.
Service Test Conducting functionalities include:
- Verifying test authorization and quota management: checks depending on the initiator of the test request if it is authorized and if limitation applies in terms of test quota (linked to resources limitation or other technical constraints)
- Carrying out test priority: the priority of a test for customer care directly in front of a user is higher than for scheduled bulk test campaign
- Managing test scheduling:
  - can be triggered to schedule test (unitary, bulk) regularly (every week, every month)
  - should take into account the impact of the test on the services in order to limit it. For example, a test on a TV service can be programmed at 2 am in order to not disturb the usage of the user.
- Managing bulk test: as well as unitary test with flexibility concerning the criteria (category of service, geographical area, type of customer)
- and setting up the test context and configuration
- Test execution with access to the various data required to perform service testing
  - During the execution of a service test, resource tests can be executed to recover information (like inventory data)  needed to calculate the service test result
  - Data collected during service test can be inventory data (for example in IS inventory) or data related to services coming from service platform and equipment like network equipment or user devices.
  - Test execution produces a test result that may contain a raw result and an enrichment of this result depending on the service test definition and associated rules.
   For example: for a internet test the raw result can be the associated bandwidth and an enrichment can be done depending on the user access type (fiber, xdsl) to evaluate if the result is suitable or not.
- Managing service test scenario: service test management must manage service test scenario with the capacity to trigger additional service tests depending on the results of previous executed tests of the test scenario.
  - In addition to rules, test scenario can be designed to use signature templates data to identify the existence of disruptive events on services that usually correspond to an aggregation of disruptions on resources like water on cable, electrical interferences with other equipment</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Test Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Test Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Test Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Repository Management Aggregate Function manages tests of services including the scheduling, setting up the test configuration, acquisition and management of test resources, test results interpretation, reporting of test results and close down of the test of the service.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Test Reporting -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Test Reporting">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Test Reporting</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Reporting reports the raw test results that may depends on the test execution context data.

	- For the same test, depending on the initiator, the result restitution can be more or less detailed. Ex: Customer care needs more and more details with higher care level.
	- However test result restitution levels must always be mutually consistent through all the execution context
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Configuration & Activation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Configuration & Activation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Configuration & Activation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Configuration &amp; Activation is in charge of 

	- configuring Know-Hows (Customer Facing Service) and Technical Solutions (Resource Facing Service) taking into account constraints between them and applying engineering rules.
	-  and activating and pre-activating Services.
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Activation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Activation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Activation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Activation Aggregate Function is in charge of activating and pre-activating Services.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Configuration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Configuration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Configuration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Configuration Aggregate Function is in charge of configuring Know-Hows (CustomerFacingService) and Technical Solutions (ResourceFacingService) taking into account constraints between them and applying engineering rules.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Order Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Order Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Order Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Order Management (SOM) Aggregate Function manages the end to end lifecycle of a Service Order request, from its configuration up to the technical delivery on network equipment and service platforms.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Availability -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Availability">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Availability</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Availability Aggregate Function validates that the service or services specified on the Service Order, or necessary regarding the product items specified in the Customer Order are:
- available at the specified customer/service location 
- feasible from a network and/or service platforms point of view
- compatible with related existing services - with or without changing their characteristics or technical solution
It includes Technical Feasibility (line type characteristics, coverage, equipment features ), and Technical Compatibility with the resources configurations already existing to support current customer installed services (refer to the Resource Availability function).

It can identify the need 
- for changing the current technical solution to respond to the order - according to allowed operations declared at catalogue level and engineering rules (Example: for an existing VOIP service, change H323 solution to SIP solution to add a new service such as Stop Secret)
- for modifying existing installed Products characteristics to maintain global integrity of a set of inter-depending services (Example: when changing the installed address of a fixed access, it can be necessary to change the call number of the related voice service, if it depends on geography)
- for ordering pre-requisite customer equipment (Example: the availability for broadband access is confirmed but the only possible - or preferred - solution is fiber, and its necessary to install an ONT and to simultaneously order a compatible box)

It also determines the standard delivery time needed, taking into account 
- resources availability
- the need of human intervention at any point of the network - and time and skills necessary to plan it
If an intervention is needed at the delivery address, regarding network context, it specifies it to the customer order capture, to allow it to manage an appointment.

To be noticed: if an intervention at the delivery address is always mandatory (or never necessary) for an ordered product, this information can be managed directly at product catalogue level.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Order Completion -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Order Completion">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Order Completion</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The service Order Completion Aggregate Function permits to enrich the CFS Order with information useful for the update of the installed resources, but not directly part of the ordered service (CFS).
Example: credentials to deliver a VOIP line, or IMSI to deliver a mobile line.
It can also describe necessary actions on other CFS, to maintain global integrity of a set of inter-depending services. 
Example: update the CFS Stop Secret - at least one of the related installed resources - when changing the call number of the CFS VOIP.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Order Initialization -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Order Initialization">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Order Initialization</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Order Initialization Aggregate Function permits to:
- Identify the CFS specification and configuration related to the ordered product, in a context of customer order delivery
- Check if a corresponding CFS is operational in the service repository, and so determine the operation at CFS level (creation or modification)
- Initiate a service (CFS) order with these information
- Eventually group in the same service order several ordered product, based on the same CFS specification, and/or identified to be delivered at the same time by the Customer Order Orchestration.

This function can also permit to initialise CFS Orders for operator internal needs, for example to change (part of) a technical solution and migrate operational CFS on the new solution elements (ex: VOIP H323 -&gt; VOIP SIP).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Order Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Order Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Order Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Order Repository Management Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to the Service (CFS) Order and to its items (e.g.: service order lines).
It gathers Service Order items delivery events from Service Order Orchestration and manages related Service Order and CFS lifecycles (via the Service Management Aggregate Function).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Order Orchestration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Order Orchestration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Order Orchestration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Order Orchestration function provides workflow and orchestration capabilities for a dedicated Service (CFS) Order.
Orchestration is needed when:
- the technical solution includes the update of the operator Installed Resources and the purchase of a partner product (ex: local loop purchase)
- a work order is necessary at the delivery address or somewhere in the operator network
- part of the delivery process or checks are delegated to another SOM (and factory) (ex : check access or update it)
- contributing or support systems have to be informed (ex : Karma for the box)

Example: to deliver a VOIP service, it will orchestrate actions on Access Network Factory (to check access compatibility), VOIP service platform and CPE.

Service Order Orchestration will also orchestrate and manage dependencies between related Service Order items of Service Order.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Technical Solution Identification -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Technical Solution Identification">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Technical Solution Identification</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Technical Solution Identification Aggregate Function identifies the technical solution to apply (RFS Specification and/or Supplier Product Specification) to deliver the ordered service (CFS) - up to the resources types needed (Resources Specification) and related operations - according to: 
- the configuration and/or location of the ordered service (CFS),
- the articulation between CFS and RFS described in the Service specification repository,
- the articulation between CFS operations and RFS operations described in the Service specification repository,
- the potential engineering rules of choice if several RFS can fit in with the CFS, as described in the Service specification repository, 
- the potential constraints related to the existing - or simultaneously ordered - customer equipment type,
- the services and resources repository.

The service inventory keeps the links between the CFS and each chosen element of the technical solution - RFS specification and resource specification or Supplier Product specification.
This function can also be used in a study phase for the design of non standard services, mainly for B2B market needs, and the definition of a new technical solution, more often by assembling elements of existing technical solutions (and adding specific equipments).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Installed Resources Identification -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Installed Resources Identification">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Installed Resources Identification</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Installed Resources Identification Aggregate Function identifies the installed resources to update, as part of the chosen technical solution, to deliver the ordered service (CFS).
This information enrich the CFS order and the CFS.

This choice is based on service catalogue rules (between RFS specification and Resources specification) and it can be necessary to check the installed resources availability, occupancy,  via the Resource Availability function.

Cloud example: In case of a cloud service, the Service Order Delivery process only identifies the equipment in charge of the management of the cloud infrastructure - and it will be informed of the ordered service characteristics. Depending on its own rules, the cloud infrastructure manager will decide or not to immediately allocate and configure all - or part of - the required resources.
So the first usage request of the cloud service can trigger the effective choice and configuration of the necessary resources - and these resources can change between usages. In this case the end of the delivery process is assumed by the manager of the cloud infrastructure. 

Remark: This function is globally a part of the technical solution identification.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Order Delivery Preparation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Order Delivery Preparation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Order Delivery Preparation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Order Delivery Preparation Aggregate Function allows in the context of a Service Order to:
- query catalogues and installed bases to identify what needs to be delivered: resource specification and its configuration, service specification (CFSSpec), intervention (WorkSpec), supplier product, related to the ordered service,
- prepare a Resource Order (CFS Order), a Service Order which will be delegated to another system, a Supplier Order, Stock Item Order or Work Order with the necessary information (the effective update in the order repositories will be supported by the corresponding Order Inventory Management functions)

In the case of a Service associated with an existing Internal Resource Type, this function also allows to: 
- check if a corresponding Installed Resource is operational in the resource installed base, and determine the operation to be performed at Resource level (creation or modification)
- eventually group in the same Resource order several ordered services, based on the same CFS specification, and/or identified to be delivered at the same time by the Service Order Delivery Orchestration.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Delivery Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Delivery Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Delivery Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Delivery Management AF groups functions involved in delivering Service Instances (such as the stream of a video, managing a customer's instance on an online game, operating a Platform-as-a-Service virtual machine, etc.).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Problem Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Problem Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Problem Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Problem Management groups functions in charge of managing Service Trouble.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Problem Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Problem Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Problem Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Problem Repository Management Aggregate Function includes:
- Service Trouble Ticket recording
- Initialization of the Service Trouble - or association to an existing Service Trouble in case of collective disturbance
- Service Trouble Ticket lifecycle management
- Reporting</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Problem Resolution -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Problem Resolution">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Problem Resolution</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Problem Resolution Aggregate Function allows to resolve the service problem in order to bring back the service to a normal operational state as efficiently as possible.
This may involve automated or manual restoration activities. It may also involve reconfiguration, re-assignments, temporary work-arounds, and/or dispatches of field technicians.
These resolution actions can be temporary or palliative, to quickly stop the trouble, and followed by other actions which will definitely treat the trouble cause. So different resolution actions can be planned and executed, associated to the same Service Trouble.
These actions can be treated through resource trouble ticket - and complementary analysis at resource level - or directly through resource order (ex: reconfiguration of an equipment).
For example to resolve a Service Trouble, the action could be to position the service on another resource (new resource order), if the current installed base resource is defective.

The Service Problem Resolution Aggregate Function includes:
- Identification of the diagnostic actions relevant to identify the service trouble, based on the service type and rules defined by the Service Problem Strategy and Policy Management function
- Triggering of these diagnostic actions and analysis of their results
- Initialization of the Service Trouble - or association to an existing Service Trouble in case of collective disturbance
- Identification of the internal actor or team in charge of the Service Trouble Ticket management, follow up and reporting
- Escalation procedure.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Problem Policy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Problem Policy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Problem Policy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Problem Policy Management Aggregate Function allows to manage the service problem typology, actions and rules, describing:
- all the types of known troubles related to a service type  
- the actions and rules of triggering, of linking and of results analysis that define the strategies for identifying
   - the trouble associated to a - or a set of - service trouble ticket(s) 
   - the cause(s) of this trouble and the actions to do to resolve the service trouble . 

The definition of the trouble (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the sequence logic of the actions to be realized to identify and qualify the trouble, one or several associated causes , and to identify the best solution to solve the problem. Specifications and rules may take into account characteristics like urgency level, impact level, priority level of the service trouble ticket.

The different action types are:
- Diagnostic actions and rules to identify the service trouble 
   - For example: simple Service test or Service test scenario, inventories consultation to identify installed service and associated installed resources, test historic consultation, 
- Diagnostic actions  and rules to identify the service trouble cause(s) and the possible root cause
   - For example: Plan workforce intervention to check an equipment, launch test scenario, query QoS historic, followed by service test launch if the installed service has a decreasing QoS, query service usage historic, service platforms logs, equipments logs, anomaly historic, 
- Definition of the logic of interpretation of the results of these diagnostic actions, to identify resolution actions. These resolution actions can be temporary or palliative, to quickly stop the trouble, and followed by other actions which will definitely treat the trouble cause. 
   - Example: to immediately solve an internet access break, we can provide the customer with a specific equipment which permits wireless connection on 3G/4G network - so he can wait for its internet access repair. 
- Definition of notification action, depending on the trouble or the trouble cause identified, for example display a message on the CSP web site, or broadcast a vocal message on IVR in case of collective trouble.
- Definition of lead action, mainly the need or not to initialize ProductTrouble Tickets for all the installed product concerned by the service trouble.

These different actions will be authorized - or not - depending on actors role and associated rights. 

It is also possible to define rules to manage Service Trouble Tickets and Service Trouble priorities, depending on SLA products subscribed (or assigned by regulatory authorities, to hospitals, strategic installations, ).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Trouble Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Trouble Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Trouble Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Trouble Analysis function permits to identify
- the service trouble cause via diagnostic actions (Test, inventories and historics consultation, Workforce Intervention) and interpretation rules of their results
- the necessary resolution actions - or the best identified solution - to realize to solve the problem.

The identification of the service trouble cause can be made through:
- Analysis of associated Service Trouble Ticket Information (first diagnostic)
- Analysis of Service alarm events (fault of performance data )
- Issuing service tests, including one time tests or a series of tests over a period of time
- Analysis of previous service test results , service usage historic, QoS historic, service platform logs, equipment logs, anomaly historics, etc.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Trouble Tracking and Business Value Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Trouble Tracking and Business Value Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Trouble Tracking and Business Value Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Trouble Tracking and Lifecycle Management Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to the Service Trouble (for example when the resolution is ended), and assures that service troubles are assigned, coordinated, efficiently solved, and escalating as needed.

It is also in charge of grouping Service Troubles with the same Trouble Cause and of grouping the related Service Trouble Tickets (for example, in case of a collective disturbance).

It manages Service Trouble closure according to business rules:

	- the service trouble can be closed as soon as a palliative action has been done and the user retrieved a not degraded use - at least one Resource Trouble Ticket is still ongoing
	- or only when the trouble is definitely treated - all associated Resource Trouble Tickets are closed.
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Quality and Performance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Quality and Performance Management Aggregated Function groups functions that supervise service functioning and manage resulting alarms.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Quality and Performance Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Quality and Performance Analysis Aggregate Function assures that service alarms produced by the Service Quality &amp; Performance Supervision function are correctly handled, affected and distributed and escalated as needed, depending on rules defined by Service Supervision Strategy &amp; Policy Management. 

The service alarms can concern 
- a bad functioning of a service (unsuccessful communications)
- the reach of a quality or performance threshold
- or a degradation of the performance or quality of service.

 Service Quality and Performance Analysis Aggregate Function includes:
- service Alarm qualification (severity level determination, enrichment with data from Service Installed Base
- filtering: if the service alarm concerns a service based on a resource currently under a scheduled work, it can be inhibited
- correlation: for example several alarms can be associated each other based on engineering rules and reduced to one root alarm on the service
- assignment and distribution of service qualified alarms if necessary to a responsible actor.

If the service alarm is validated and requires a search for cause and a triggering of action, a service trouble ticket is created and will be handled by trouble mechanism. When the Service Trouble is resolved, the Service Alarm could be, automatically or not, deactivated or its severity level decreased.

In other cases the alarm can be handled, without creating an associated trouble ticket, by following exploitation forms, with possibly triggering of manual actions.
In every cases, it could be closed manually by an assigned actor.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Quality and Performance Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Quality and Performance Repository Management Aggregate Function collects and manages Service quality and performance from Resource quality and performance and from Service tests.
It is also in charge of reporting Service Quality and Performance.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Supervision -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Supervision">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Quality and Performance Supervision</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Quality and Performance Supervision Aggregate Function enables service functioning to be measured (in real-time or not), and supervised, depending on rules defined by Service Quality and Performance Strategy Development, in order to prevent the service quality and performance degradation, with an on-the-fly analysis. 

It includes: 
- filtering, aggregation of the collected information
- analysis of 
     - different types of information directly issued from equipment 
          - examples: continuity check information, IP ping, time to access Internet services transmitted by probes, 
     - results of tests or historic of tests
     - service usage
     - traffic degradations. 
- it compares these information with expected values or pre-established limits
     - when there is a great augmentation of service use and traffic congestion it can trigger an alarm that will be handled via alarm &amp; problem management to set up a load-shedding mechanism (example for new year SMS).
- enrich or calculate service quality and performance indicators (includes KQI, KPI) 
     - Examples of KQI: time to repair warranty, availability percentage on a time slot, percentage of correct vocal quality communications
- it compares these indicators with expected values or pre-established limits
- analysis and evaluation of service performance indicators evolution
- it enables the quality of services to be analyzed by means of aggregation, enrichment, and correlation of performance data.
- it produces Service Alarm 
     - in case of performance degradation (for example when a predefined threshold is reached in application of the rules defined at the strategy level)
     - in case of SLA violations 
- it manages (generation/visualization/distribution) the reports resulting of QoS analysis.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Usage Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Usage Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Usage Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Usage Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of collecting, identifying, aggregating and assigning Service Usage records.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Service Usage Guiding -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Service Usage Guiding">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Service Usage Guiding</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Service Usage Guiding Aggregate Function ensures resource usage records aggregation (consolidation, elimination of duplicates) to generate service usage records ("global usage repor" often called Charging Data Record or CDR).
Each service usage record is associated to the installed service that generated it (ex: mobile line service).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Strategic Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Strategic Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Strategic Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Strategic Analysis Aggregated Function groups functions useful to conduct delayed analysis, on the collected information concerning resource, which may have an influence on the strategy.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Infrastructure Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Infrastructure Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Infrastructure Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Infrastructure Analysis Aggregate Function analysis Network infrastructure for identifying optimum network deployments, identifying strategic sites and Network sizing needs.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Supply Chain Planning Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Supply Chain Planning Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Supply Chain Planning Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Supply Chain Planning Analysis Aggregate Function identifies sourcing forecast and plans sourcing taking into account current inventory, in collaboration with all suppliers.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Performance Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Performance Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Performance Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Performance Analysis Aggregate Function comprises off-line analysis of the resource performance and forecast concerning the resource performance indicators, depending on rules and indicators defined through Resource Supervision Strategy and Policy Management.

It calculates resource performance indicators (aggregated or not) and produces statistics and forecasts concerning the resource performance (ex: loads linked to the uses, number of failures, ), based on the following information: 
- indicators already calculated by the Resource Performance Supervision function
- aggregated resource usages 
- historic of resource test
- resource trouble or resource trouble ticket
- resource alarm
- data directly issued from equipment - all of them are not analyzed on the fly.

The information produced can also be used to improve the rules and indicators definition of Resource Supervision Policy Management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage a quality loop on diagnostic to continually tweak and improve it.
Functionalities include:
- Management of success/failure result feedback of diagnostic and correction provided by technicians or the systems after a correction
- Offline analysis of the success/failure data compared to the diagnostic and correction rules to provide modifications to improve them. This analysis is done using statistical diagnostic data and test results to evaluate their relevance and the needed updates.
The provided improvements can be used to update the definition of diagnostic rules and correction actions in the Resource Trouble Policy Management function and the definition of tests in the Resource Test Policy Management function.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Strategy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Strategy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Strategy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Strategy Management aggregate function groups functions useful to define the resource strategy and to develop it (new resources or enhance the existing resources).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Strategy Design and Planning -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Strategy Design and Planning">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Strategy Design and Planning</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Strategy, Design and Planning is responsible for the definition of the resource strategy at the company level. This function takes into account a list of inputs to determine the need to implement new resources, to modify or replace existing resources or to remove them. As inputs, the function can use the following information: 
- The existing context of the company with available services and resources (network, PFS)
- The data produced by the Resource Usage Analysis function and traffic and geographical evolution analyses
- The performance and test results of existing resources or in test resources (like POC to validate a new technology)
- The new technologies that are developed in the industry 
- Marketing constraints concerning the date of opening for a new service that requires evolution at the resource level 
- The cost of the existing and new technologies with benefit evaluation 
- The possibility to share resources with third parties/partner 
- The service strategy already defined in a top down process (service driven)

Following the definition of the service and resource strategy the Resource Strategy, Design and Planning function updates the resource design by requesting the modifications (creating, updating, deleting) of the resource specifications to the resource catalogue and thus the evolution of the available technology solutions.

Finally, the Resource Strategy, Design and Planning function provides a high level planning of the resource strategy implementation taking into account the service strategy plan. The strategy planning can take into account constraints (like legal constraints or the need of validation by authorities) and notify other companies, local authorities or governments of the chosen strategy, for example the choice of FTTH technology versus VDSL.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Capability Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Capability Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Capability Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Capability Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of specifying and managing Resource Specification capabilities.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Specification Capability Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Specification Capability Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Specification Capability Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Capability Specification Management Aggregate Function manages Resource Capability Specifications for all Resource Specifications. The capability specifications represent the general, common and invariant characteristics of resource that may be realized in more than one type of specific resource. Examples of capability are Layer2, Data, radio and Transport.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Deployment and Retirement -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Deployment and Retirement">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Deployment and Retirement</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Deployment and Retirement Aggregate Function is responsible for implementing the resource strategy high level plan and develop the resource infrastructure (network, PFS, IT...). The Resource Deployment and Retirement function manages the deployment constraints like area limitation (for test or for marketing purposes), progressive deployment following precise milestones These constrains may be provided from the service level.

The resource deployment and retirement can be responsible to manage constraints (like legal constraints or the need of validation by authorities) and notify other companies, local authorities or governments of the effective deployment or retirement of resources like the implementation of FTTH technology in a building.

The Resource Installed Base is updated and the resources are pre-configured and pre-activated if needed, for example for non-customer specific parameters, via the Resource Order Initialization and Installed Resource Management functions.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Test Resources Check -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Test Resources Check">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Test Resources Check</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Resources Check Aggregate Function is in charge of defining, implementing and checking resources used to test Resources.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Network Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Network Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Network Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Development AF groups functions that acquire, expand, improve and maintain communications networks.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Network Planning and Building -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Network Planning and Building">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Network Planning and Building</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Planning and Building AF groups functions responsible for ensuring network capacity is available to meet the needs of Customer service requests and ensuring network restorability. Network capacity includes the access network and the various OSI Layer 1-4+ core backbone networks. Network Planning and Building functions manage network capacity with the goals of (1) maximizing return from capital expenditures, (2) ensuring inventory and financial accuracy, (3) providing capacity to meet customer demands, (4) future-proofing evolving technology options, (5) best leveraging of supply-chain management, (6) setting up the OSS data to permit flow-through automation once network elements have been made available in OSS inventory.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Network Access Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Network Access Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Network Access Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Access Management AF groups functions that investigate and implement alternatives to building network.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Network Change Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Network Change Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Network Change Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Network Change Management AF groups functions that notify and communicate to concerned parties about changes to the communications network and its elements - planned changes, changes happening in real-time, and past changes (for root cause analysis) - in order to reduce the "blast radius" of such changes</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Specification Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Specification Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Specification Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Specification Management Aggregated Function functions enable to create and update the catalogue at Resource level, for all types of resources (ResourceSpecification):
- Physical Resources (ex: cable, passive equipment, )
- Logical Resources (ex: phone number, IP address, APPS such as Orange TV, Orange Maps, )
- Compound Resources (ex: DSLAM, box, )
It manages the links with the Business Partner Product Catalogue for Resources specification that correspond to equipment rented to a supplier (devices, network equipment, hardware &amp; software, ).
It also manages the links to the Skill catalogue to identify for each type of resource - or of operation on a resource - which type of skill is necessary to make the intervention.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Allocation Policy Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Allocation Policy Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Allocation Policy Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Allocation Policy Development Aggregate Function enables to define and to check consistency of potentially complex rules to automate the choice of resource type and/or instance during different processes such as delivery management, assurance, infrastructure management, . 

Criteria can be of various nature and combined:
- geographical (for fixed phone number allocation, )
- defined by regulation authorities which allocate the resources to the Service Provider (range of MSISDN dedicated to M2M, IMSI allocation rules to SIM cards depending of future vendor - the Service Provider itself, MVNO,  - , duration of freezing before using again a phone number, )
- depending on the configured characteristics of the Installed Service / Resource to deliver (ex: phone number allocated during Customer Order Capture, internet speed required, ) or associated to it (ex: QoS or SLA)
- depending on the current state of Service Platforms, Network equipment (occupation rate, load balancing,  )
- depending on  Supplier/Partner contracts (volume discounts, )</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Specification Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Specification Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Specification Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Specification Development Aggregate Function enables to create and update the catalogue at Resource level, for all types of resources (Resource Specification):
- Physical Resources (ex: cable, passive equipment, )
- Logical Resources (ex: phone number, IP address, APPS such as TV, Maps, )
- Compound Resources (ex: DSLAM, box, )
Examples of Resource Specification:
- iphone 5S and its characteristics such as colors available, size and weight, capacity, 
- DSLAM Alcatel XXX and its characteristics such as size and weight, electricity consumption, number of DSL ports, 

It manages the links with the Supplier/Partner Product Catalogue for Resources specification that correspond to equipment rented to a supplier (devices, network equipments, hardware &amp; software, ).
It also manages the links to the Skill catalogue to identify for each type of resource - or of operation on a resource - which type of skill is necessary to make the intervention.

It also permits to describe in a short way resources types that as a Service Provider we dont own or commercialize (ex: mobile phones used by our customers).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Test Policy and Schedule Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Test Policy and Schedule Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Test Policy and Schedule Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Policy and Schedule Management Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage the resource test catalogue containing the resource test specifications, the rules that define the test configuration setting up based on the context, the rules that define the strategies for executing the test as well as how the test results should be restituted.
Test strategies can range from simple to complex and encompass the definition of test scenario.
The definition of the test (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the question that is asked and thus shapes the result that is reported back.
The functionalities include providing:
- Resource Test Specification and test scenario management
  - Test scenario management: for example, in case of redundant resources the test scenario can test the default resources involved in a service before testing one of the possible redundant resources solutions available and not necessarily test all the other existing solutions if the previous tests are positives.
  - Managing resource test capabilities: some resources have limited capabilities for executing tests, especially for multiple parallel testing. Resources can be either the resources involved for the user or test related resources like probes &amp; robots.
  - Managing initiator authorization: some tests are restricted to some user roles (internal or external) so the authorizations are defined at the Resource Test Strategy and Policy Management.
- Test rules defining the strategies for setting up test configuration
- Test rules defining how to carrying out the resource test
  - For example: to choose the next test in a scenario depending on the results of the previous tests
- Test rules defining the enrichment of test results. 
  - For example: the enrichment of CPU &amp; disk space usage compared to the defined strategy of the company
  - For example: the enrichment of copper line electrical conductivity compared to previously measure template
- Test rules defining the restitution of the result depending on test execution context
   - For example: a test result of ok/not ok can be enough for a user whereas technicians require more details in the result of the test.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Supervision Policy Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Supervision Policy Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Supervision Policy Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Supervision Policy Development Aggregate Function enables to define the policy concerning supervision (indicators and alarm) at Resource level.

It includes: 
- Definition of indicators (measures) associated with the resource type, with thresholds, calculation rules and consequences
     - examples of indicators with threshold : pressure, temperature 
     - examples of indicators with calculation rules : storage occupation rate, load rate
- Definition of rules 
     - for alarm activation
          - for example if a dysfunction is detected in its power supply, or in the functioning of the elements which constitutes it (card, port, internal ventilator) and also if its temperature exceeds a threshold.
     - for a Trouble ticket creation next to a resource alarm qualification.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Trouble Policy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Policy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Trouble Policy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Trouble Policy Management Aggregate Function allows to manage the resource problem typology, actions and rules describing:
- all the types of known troubles related to resource type  
- the actions and rules of triggering, of linking and of results analysis that define the strategies for identifying  
   - the trouble associated to a - or a set of - resource trouble ticket(s)
   - the cause(s) of this trouble and the actions to do to resolve the resource trouble. 

The definition of the trouble (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the sequence logic of the actions to be realized to identify and qualify the problem, one or several associated causes , and to identify the best solution to solve the problem. Specifications and rules may take into account characteristics like urgency level, impact level, priority level of the resource trouble ticket.

The different action types are:
- Diagnostic actions and rules to identify the resource trouble. This step permits to identify the installed resource(s) at fault
   - For example: simple resource test or resource test scenario, inventories consultation to identify installed resource and expected configuration characteristics, test historic consultation, 
- Diagnostic actions  and rules to identify the resource trouble cause(s) and the possible root cause . This step permits to identify why the related installed resource(s) is (are) at fault.
   - For example: Plan workforce intervention to check an equipment, launch resource test scenario, query QoS historic, followed by resource test launch if the installed resource has a decreasing QoS, query resource usage historic, equipments logs, anomaly historic, .
- Definition of the logic of interpretation of the results of these diagnostic actions, to identify resolution actions. These resolution actions can be temporary or palliative, to quickly stop the trouble, and followed by other actions which will definitely treat the trouble cause 
   - For example: if a network cable is cut, we can first decide to migrate all or part of the interrupted installed services on another cable (through dedicated resource orders) to quickly solve the trouble, and secondly initiate a project - often called scheduled work - to repair the cut cable. After the final repair, its not always necessary to migrate the installed service back.
- Definition of notification action, depending on the trouble or the trouble cause identified, for example display a message on the web site, or broadcast a vocal message on IVR in case of collective trouble (example: network cable cut, power cut, ) 
- Definition of lead action, mainly the need or not to initialize Service Trouble Tickets for all the installed services concerned by the resource trouble.

These different actions will be authorized - or not - depending on actors role and associated rights. 
For example we could authorized the customer (or user) to directly diagnose a configuration problem on his box and to trigger the upload of a new software version, but reserve the capacity of updating the configuration of a service platform to support level 2 actors.

It is also possible to define rules to manage Resource Trouble Tickets and Resource Trouble priorities, depending on criteria such as:
- Priority of the related Service Trouble Ticket(s) or Service Trouble(s) 
- Number of Product Trouble Tickets that can be expected, linked to the usage of the installed resource.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Supply Chain Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Supply Chain Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Supply Chain Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Supply Chain Development groups functions useful to develop the Supply Chain (new Stock Items or existing Stock Items evolution). This also includes identifying links with associated human actors.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Stock Item Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Stock Item Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Stock Item Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Stock Item Management Aggregate Function enables to define Stock Items handled by the supply chain for stock management and shipping.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Work Specification Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Work Specification Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Work Specification Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Work Specification Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of Work specifications development.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Work Specification Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Work Specification Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Work Specification Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Work Specification Development Aggregate Function enables to specify types of Work including required skills and resources to realize human activities such as training or resource installation.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Management groups functions in charge of managing the inventory of resources.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Location Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Location Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Location Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Location Management Aggregate Function permits to describe, identify and manage any type of location useful for the Service Provider operational processes.
It includes:
- geographical address description (city, street, number in the street, ), described using national or international codifications (for streets, countries, etc.),
- buildings description (floors, rooms, racks, ), 
- geospatial information,
-Information such as contact methods, opening time, access code, warnings, etc. can also be defined.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Regulated Logical Resources Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Regulated Logical Resources Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Regulated Logical Resources Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Regulated Logical Resources Management Aggregate Function permits to describe and manage logical resources such as phone numbers, IP addresses, IMSI, frequencies  which are usually allocated to the Service Provider by regulation authorities, and often in limited quantities.
Specific rules defined by regulation authorities are treated by the Resource Allocation Policy Management function.
The Service Provider also often has specific operations to manage when these resources are allocated or returned, and fair usage reporting to ensure.
For example, this function covers Portability constraints for phone numbers, which oblige the Service Provider to always know which operator uses a phone number to be able to manage changes of operator and network routing.
It can also cover constraints related to mail address management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Allocation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Allocation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Allocation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Reconciliation Aggregate Function defines, suggests to an administrator or directly applies rules to treat gaps between the Installed Resource Base and the current state or characteristics of Installed Resources identified by the Audit - Resource Discovery function. 

These rules describe in which cases it is necessary to update the Installed Resource Base (ex: notification of a new equipment), via the Installed Resource Management function, or to update the equipment itself (ex: box)  - in some cases it can be necessary  
- to trigger again the configuration of the installed resource 
- or even to trigger human intervention (ex: gap on physical network)</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Audit and Discovery -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Audit and Discovery">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Audit and Discovery</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Audit - Resource Discovery Aggregate Function permits to identify and check the consistency of the current state and the configured characteristics of the Installed Resources, between the installed resources base (or inventory) and the equipments. Several mechanisms are available such as:
- Notification (concern active equipment, for example box, OLT, mobile phone really used, )
- Polling (concern passive equipment). Polling can be automatic or on-demand (in case of Trouble Ticket for example), direct or through intermediate equipment. For example, to identify home equipment, information is collected through the box.
- Report by technicians in the field

The installed resources identified by the Audit-Resource Discovery function can be:
- elements of the Service Provider Infrastructure
- devices sold or rented to customers by the Service Provider
- devices owned by customers or users, not sold or rented by the Service Provider, and directly or not connected to its network.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Repository Reconciliation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Repository Reconciliation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Repository Reconciliation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Repository Reconciliation Aggregate Function defines, suggests to an administrator or directly applies rules to treat gaps between the Installed Resource Base and the current state or characteristics of Installed Resources identified by the Audit - Resource Discovery function. 

These rules describe in which cases it is necessary to update the Installed Resource Base (ex: notification of a new equipment), via the Installed Resource Management function, or to update the equipment itself (ex: box)  - in some cases it can be necessary  
- to trigger again the configuration of the installed resource 
- or even to trigger human intervention (ex: gap on physical network)</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Repository Management Aggregate Function stores and maintains a reference of all technical resources (physical, logical and compound resources). 
The Resource base describes in particular network and IT resources 
- created and eventually configured during the Infrastructure Management process,
- allocated and configured during the Service Delivery process,
- or during Assurance process 
Resources (physical or compound) are linked to their geographical location, when it is relevant and stable. 
Example: DSLAM XXX is located 2 street Louis Armstrong 75015 Paris, building A, floor 2, room 214
Resources can be identified and referenced before any allocation to a service (and/or a product) - and can exist without being used, by a service for example.
Examples:
- devices managed in the Service Provider stocks
- fixed access
- new DSLAM or Service Platform
It also permits to describe in a short way resources that as a Service Provider we dont own or commercialize (ex: mobile phones used by our customers) but that we need to know or to act on. For example when someone arrives in a foreign country with his mobile phone, his SIM card is updated for roaming management (as hosting network).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Simulation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Simulation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Simulation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Simulation Aggregate Function provides capacity to design Resource infrastructure and helps analyzing potential changes.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Mediation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Mediation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Mediation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Mediation Aggregate Function provides capacity to exchange data with Resources.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Geographical Association -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Geographical Association">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Geographical Association</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Geographical Association Aggregate Function includes functions that relate Resources to points or boundaries as identified by geographical references. Geographical references could be as defined in GIS systems, or as defined by external parties or agencies (such as postal codes, emergency response zones, legal boundaries, etc.).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Test Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Test Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Test Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Management provides the necessary functionalities to manage the end-to-end execution of a test for a resource, either for manual or automatic testing in order to ensure that this resource is working properly. It includes:
- Verifying test authorization and quota management
- Carrying out test priority
- Managing test scheduling
- Managing bulk test
- Retrieval of appropriate inventory data and setting up the test context and configuration
- Test execution with access to the various resources required to perform resource testing
- Managing resource test scenario
- Test results restitution
- Test results long term logging for offline data analyses. 
- Possible reporting of test results back to the initiator of the test.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Test Conducting -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Test Conducting">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Test Conducting</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Conducting provides the necessary functionalities to manage the end-to-end execution of a test for a resource, either for manual or automatic testing in order to ensure that this resource is working properly.

The Resource Test Conducting function is used in the following processes:
- fulfillment, usually triggered within the framework of a test of service, for ensuring that the assigned resource works as designed.
- fault management, which can trigger automatic tests during resource fault isolation.
- resource supervision, performance &amp; quality management to acquire data necessary to analyze the running resources.

Resource Test Conducting functionalities include:
- Verifying test authorization and quota management: resource test management checks depending on the initiator of the test request if it is authorized and if limitation applies in terms of test quota (linked to resources limitation or other technical constraints)
- Carrying out test priority: the priority of a test for technician intervening at the user home is higher than for scheduled bulk test campaign
- Managing test scheduling:
  - Resource Test Conducting can be triggered to schedule test (unitary, bulk) regularly (every week, every month)
  - Resource Test Conducting should take into account the impact of the test on the resources in order to limit it. For example, a remote test on a user STB can be programmed at 2 am in order to not disturb the usage of the user.
- Managing bulk test: resource test management must be able to execute unitary test as bulk test with flexibility concerning the criteria (for a category of resource, geographical area, type of customer)
- Retrieval of appropriate inventory data and setting up the test context and configuration
- Test execution with access to the various resources required to perform resource testing. The test execution produces a test result that may contain a raw result and an enrichment of this result depending on the resource test definition and associated rules.
  - For example: for a copper line test the raw result can be the conductivity of the line  and an enrichment can be done depending on conductivity templates calculated previously to evaluate if the result is suitable or not.
- Managing resource test scenario: resource test management must manage resource test scenario with the capacity to trigger additional resource tests depending on the results of previous executed tests of the test scenario.
  - In addition to rules, test scenario can be designed to use signature templates data to identify the existence of disruptive events on resources like water on cable, electrical interferences with other equipment
- Test results restitution: the restitution of the raw test results may depends on the test execution context data.
  - For the same test, depending on the initiator, the result restitution can be more or less detailed. Customer care or technician (ex: during a turn on test) needs more and more details with higher care level. 
  - However test result restitution levels must always be mutually consistent through all  the execution context
- Test results long term logging for offline data analyses. These analyses are done outside of Resource Test Management.
- Possible reporting of test results back to the initiator of the test.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Test Inventory Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Test Inventory Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Test Inventory Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Inventory Management Aggregate Function manages Resource Tests designs and results.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Test Reporting -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Test Reporting">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Test Reporting</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Reporting Aggregate Function provides reporting of resource test results back to the requester.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Test Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Test Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Test Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Analysis Aggregate Function analysis Resource Tests results and forecast long term error trajectory.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Network Capacity Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Network Capacity Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Network Capacity Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Capacity Management AF includes functions that address the near-term management of network asset utilization. This includes: Monitor, provide trend analysis and direct the deployment, use and capacity of network inventory in the 0-12 month timeframe to ensure the right technology is efficiently deployed and positioned to meet the service delivery needs of the market, while minimizing capital and expense. For ports, bandwidth and memory measures, this includes measures such as the current consumption, months to exhaust, and average monthly consumption. Capacity management triggers activation of components to meet the current demand.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Work Order Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Work Order Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Work Order Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Work Order Management aggregate functions track and manage the events and the lifecycle related to Work Orders, and assures that they are assigned, coordinated and escalating as needed.
It is especially in charge of initiating re-assignments as needed, based on changing priorities, technician progress on other job assignments, etc.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Work Order Assignment and Plan -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Work Order Assignment and Plan">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Work Order Assignment and Plan</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Work Order Assignment and Plan Aggregate Function provides the functionality required to assign a WorkOrder (that corresponds to manual tasks) to staff members, and to plan it according to :
- WorkOrder priority and due date
- tasks planned duration and required skills
- tasks location - and related constraints like opening time, security issues,  , and optimization of staff trips
- workload / availability of technicians or other concerned staff members
 
There are two possible levels of assignment of a WorkOrder to a calendar entry :
- first, the Work Order is assigned to a pool calendar, according to location and skills criteria.
- secondly, the Work Order is assigned to one or several actors calendar according to real availability, tour planning (mobility management), specific location issues such as safety (e.g. presence of two people needed in some places due to the height of the equipments, and so on)
In case of subcontracting, the operator only performs the first level of assignment, according to the agreement with the supplier.
Back office activities too are only assigned at team level, and then ensure by team members through the team basket.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Work Order Establishment -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Work Order Establishment">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Work Order Establishment</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Work Order Establishment Aggregate Function receives requests which imply to plan and assign manual activities to staff in a workforce. This work request can be produced from various systems such as Interaction Management, Order Management, Problem Management, Infrastructure Management, etc.
The activity can correspond to technical tasks, an appointment with a customer for pre-sales or demo, 

This function includes :
- filtering the various requests (several requests may result in a single job to be performed, emitters may send duplicate demands, requested action may already be identified by Problem Management, )
- choice of the carrying out model and decomposing the request into Work Order(s) and Work Order Item(s) - each Work Order describing all the operations to be performed by the same actor type at the same time in the same location.
- adding context information (e.g. location information and associated constraints (opening hours/security issues) and produces Work Order(s) to perform the initial request.

The request can be described by one of the following : 
- Resource Order: The order provides the initial state of the resources and the requested final state/or the operation to be executed on them. The Resource Order may be issued by a Customer Order or a Service Order, by an Internal production process, by a problem management restoration process. 
- Project Item: i.e. a specific task in a planned project such as infrastructure roll-out, software deployment, B2B customer complex installation, preventive maintenance, ...
- Interaction: i.e. any agreement to manage an appointment between a company representative (sales, technician, ) and a customer representative for pre-sales, demo, etc.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Work Order Tracking and Business Value Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Work Order Tracking and Business Value Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Work Order Tracking and Business Value Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Work Order Tracking and Business Value Development Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to Work Orders, and assures that they are assigned, coordinated and escalating as needed.

It is especially in charge of initiating re-assignments as needed, based on changing priorities, technician progress on other job assignments, etc.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Network Performance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Network Performance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Network Performance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Network Performance Management aggregate function ensures the efficiency and reliability of a CSPs network infrastructure. Its responsibilities can be summarized in the following key points:
1.	Monitoring Network Health: Continuously track network performance and identify issues.
2.	Fault Detection: Quickly detect and diagnose network faults or failures.
3.	Performance Optimization: Optimize network configurations and resources for efficiency.
4.	Bandwidth Management: Ensure proper allocation of bandwidth to meet user needs.
5.	Security Monitoring: Monitor for potential security threats and vulnerabilities.
6.	Capacity Planning: Forecast network growth and plan for scalability.
7.	Reporting and Analytics: Provide data-driven insights for decision-making.
8.	Quality of Service (QoS): Ensure consistent and reliable service quality.
9.	Incident Management: Rapidly respond to and resolve network incidents.
10.	Compliance: Ensure network operations align with regulatory and organizational standards, such as SLAs.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Order Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Order Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Order Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Order Management functions manage the end to end lifecycle of a Resource Order request.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Availability Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Availability Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Availability Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Availability Management Aggregate Function ensures that a resource - or a set of resources - is available to deliver a service (CFS) order for example. It can check:
- the state and current configuration of the resource(s)
- its occupancy
- its location
- for an existing local loop if it is directly reusable from one end to the other, or partially un-built (and where it's necessary to rebuild)

This Aggregate Function can be used to confirm Service Availability (at least one set of needed resources is available) and/or the choice of the resources as part of the technical solution.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Order Completion -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Order Completion">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Order Completion</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Order Completion Aggregate Function permits to enrich the Resource Order with information needed by the equipment, but which are not part of the description of the asked action and havent to be known by the requester of this action. 
Example: the name of the holder or user may be needed by a service platform to deliver a CFS.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Order Initialization -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Order Initialization">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Order Initialization</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">A Resource Order describes actions required on resources, and can be initiated in several contexts:
- by a Resource Order Management component to update the equipments required to deliver/activate a CFS, or modify a technical solution element
- by the Supply Chain to answer to the customer order of a goods product
- by the Infrastructure Management process, to install new equipments
- by the Service &amp; Resource Assurance process to diagnose or resolve a trouble</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Order Orchestration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Order Orchestration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Order Orchestration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Order Orchestration Aggregate Function orchestrates the actions on the involved resources, managing dependencies between them.
Example: for SIP VOIP delivery, depending of the chosen technical solution, the Resource Order Management component can orchestrate the update of the HSS, of the AS BASE and of the ENUM platform.
It manages rollback, for example of the updates if one of the equipments cant be updated, and prepares the notification with the global result of the Resource Order (OK or NOK).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Order Tracking and Business Value Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Order Tracking and Business Value Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Order Tracking and Business Value Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Order Tracking and Business Value Development Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to the Resource Order and to its items (e.g.: order lines). 
It gathers Resource Order items delivery events from Resource Order Orchestration and manages related Resource Order lifecycle, and Installed Resources lifecycle (via the Installed Resource Management function).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Supply Chain Fulfillment -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Supply Chain Fulfillment">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Supply Chain Fulfillment</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Supply Chain Fulfillment aggregate function groups functions related to stock movement, logistics, inventory control, assembling process.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Stock Inventory Control -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Stock Inventory Control">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Stock Inventory Control</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Stock Inventory Control Aggregate Function performs physical inventory of entire inventory or specific sub inventories and defines its approval cycles.
It includes Inventory Quality Control Management dedicated to the control of quality of product in receiving and return processes.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Stock Inventory Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Stock Inventory Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Stock Inventory Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Stock Inventory Management Aggregate Function is in charge of managing Stock Items with the related Resources, stock level and requests and tracks Stock Items with its Resources in Warehouses according to receptions.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Stock Level Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Stock Level Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Stock Level Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Stock Level Management Aggregate Function is in charge of Warehouse and partners replenishment identification. 
The replenishment business rules can concern materials provided by suppliers or materials distributed within the company (point of sales or warehouses).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Stock Movements Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Stock Movements Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Stock Movements Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Stock Movements Management Aggregate Function manages items movements (i.e. shipment). It includes transfers and reallocations within the organization or to partners.
It's in charge of shipment realization and follow-up including when it is directly done by a business partner.
Examples: This function is used when devices are delivered in shop, or when an equipment is replaced by a technician during an intervention.
Stock Movements Management also manages the stock of documentation and type of documentation (Commercial catalogue, flyers etc.).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Logistic Availability -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Logistic Availability">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Logistic Availability</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Logistic Availability Aggregate Function allows to:
- Check the availability of tangible products,
- Get the delivery duration,
- Book/release a tangible product.

For this function, the supply chain uses its own stock and delivery duration, but also the stock and delivery duration of its partners.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Provisioning -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Provisioning">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Provisioning</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Provisioning Aggregate Function includes functions that design, set up, assign, configure and activate (or support the design, setup, assignment, configuration and activation) of resource solutions, whether in fulfillment of service orders or maintenance or build-out of the communications network.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Provisioning Monitoring -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Provisioning Monitoring">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Provisioning Monitoring</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Provision Monitoring Aggregate Function includes functions that oversee and track Resource Provisioning activities.  Resource Provisioning includes the design, set up, assign, configure and activate (or support the design, setup, assignment, configuration and activation) of resource solutions, whether in fulfillment of service orders or maintenance or build-out of the communications network.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Configuration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Configuration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Configuration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Configuration Aggregate Function is in charge of configuring Resources taking into account constraints between them and applying engineering rules.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Activation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Activation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Activation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Activation Aggregate Function is in charge of activating and pre-activating Resources.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Provisioning Design -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Provisioning Design">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Provisioning Design</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Provisioning Design aggregate function groups functions that support the design of resource-based solutions, whether standard (for implementation of product specifications) or custom (for customer-specific services).  The design identifies what resources will need to provisioned and how.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Performance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Performance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Performance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Performance Management Aggregated Function groups functions that supervise resource functioning and manage resulting alarms.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Alarm Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Alarm Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Alarm Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Alarm Repository Management Aggregate Function is in charge of managing Resource Alarms including recording, lifecycle management and reporting.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Alarm Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Alarm Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Alarm Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">This Resource Alarm Management Aggregate Function assures that resource alarms produced are correctly handled, affected and distributed and escalated as needed, depending on rules defined by Resource Strategy and Policy Management. 

The resource alarm can concern 
- the functioning of a resource (power, card, temperature dysfunction)
- the reach of a performance threshold 
- or a performance degradation. 

This function includes: 
- qualification: severity level determination, enrichment with data from Resource Installed Base.
- filtering: if the resource alarm concern an equipment currently under a scheduled work, it is inhibited
- correlation: for example several alarms can be associated each other based on engineering rules and reduced to one root alarm on the resource
- assignment and distribution of resource qualified alarms if necessary to a responsible actor.

If the resource alarm is recognized and requires a search for cause and triggering of action, a resource trouble ticket is created and will be handled by trouble mechanism.
When the resource Trouble is resolved, the Resource Alarm could be, automatically or not, deactivated or its severity level decreased.

In other cases the alarm can be handled, without creating an associated trouble ticket, by following exploitation forms, with possibly triggering of manual actions.
In every case, it could be closed manually by an assigned actor.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Performance Supervision -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Performance Supervision">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Performance Supervision</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Performance Supervision Aggregate Function enables resource functioning to be measured and supervised (in real-time or not), depending on rules defined by Resource Strategy Management, in order to prevent the resource performance degradation, with an on-the-fly analysis. 

It includes: 
- filtering, aggregation
- analysis of 
     - different types of information directly issued from equipment 
          - examples: usages, logs, flows, 
     -  results of tests or historic of tests
- it compares these information with expected values or pre-established limits
- enrich or calculate resource performance indicators (KPI) 
     - examples: Mean Time to Repair (MTTR), Mean Time Between Failure (MTBF)
- analysis and evaluation of resource performance indicators evolution
- it produces Resource Alarm 
     - when an information doesnt correspond to the expected value
     - threshold correlation: when a predefined threshold is exceeded, it is able to trigger a performance alarm which can be managed by Resource Alarm Management function.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Performance Planning -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Performance Planning">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Performance Planning</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Performance Planning AF groups functions optimizing an organization's resources effectively. Their primary responsibilities include:
1.	Forecasting resource demands based on historical data and future needs.
2.	Allocating resources efficiently to meet operational requirements.
3.	Monitoring resource utilization and identifying bottlenecks.
4.	Ensuring cost-effective resource utilization to meet business objectives.
5.	Implementing scheduling and routing techniques to maximize resource efficiency.
6.	Adapting plans long-range and in real-time to address changing demands or disruptions.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Trouble Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Trouble Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Trouble Management Aggregate Function groups functions that supervise resource functioning and manage resource troubles of any kind.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Trouble Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Trouble Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString"></rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Trouble Resolution -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Resolution">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Trouble Resolution</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Trouble Resolution Aggregate Function is responsible for repair or replacement of faulty resources or using redundant equipment to replace the faulty resource after a trouble has been detected, in order to bring back the installed resource to a normal operational state as efficiently as possible.  
This may involve automated or manual restoration activities. It may also involve reconfiguration, and/or dispatches of field technicians.
These resolution actions can be temporary or palliative, to quickly stop the trouble, and followed by other actions which will definitely treat the trouble cause (ex: when a telegraph pole falls it can be first strengthened then replaced). So different resolution actions can be planned and executed, associated to the same Trouble. 
When we try palliative action and notice that it doesnt solve the trouble, its not always necessary or interesting to undo it (ex: change of copper pair).
These actions can be treated through resource order(s) (ex: reconfiguration of an equipment, installation of a new equipment) or project management (ex: scheduled work) when several tasks have to be planned and coordinated.
Remark: If the resolution action is a Resource Order, this will be created via the Resource Order Initialization function.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Trouble Ticket Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Ticket Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Trouble Ticket Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">A Resource Trouble Ticket can be created 
- further to a Product Trouble Ticket resulting from the qualification of a problem indicated by a customer on a material product, 
- or to a Service Trouble Ticket when the resource(s) responsible for the service trouble is (are) identified
- or following resource validated alarms  
- or QoS decreasing validated report

The Resource Trouble Ticket Management Aggregate Function includes:
-  Resource Trouble Ticket recording
- Identification of the diagnostic actions relevant to identify the resource trouble, depending on the resource type and rules defined by the Resource Problem Strategy and Policy Management function
- Triggering of these diagnostic actions and analysis of their results
- Initialization of the Resource Trouble - or association to an existing Resource Trouble
- Identification of the internal or external actor or team in charge of the Resource
- Trouble Ticket management, follow up and reporting
- Resource Trouble Ticket lifecycle management
- Reporting 
- Escalation procedure.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Trouble Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Trouble Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Trouble Analysis Aggregate Function permits, to identify 
- the trouble causes - that is to say the reasons why the installed resource is in trouble and among these reasons the root cause of the resource trouble 
- and the necessary resolution actions to solve the problem.

It is based on diagnostic actions (Test, inventories and historic consultation, workforce intervention) and rules defined by Resource Problem Strategy and Policy Management. 

The identification of the resource trouble cause can be made through:
- Analysis of associated Service Trouble Ticket Information (first diagnostic)
- Analysis of associated Resource Trouble Ticket Information (first diagnostic)
- Analysis of Resource alarm events (fault of performance data )
- Issuing resource tests, including one time tests or a series of tests over a period of time
- Analysis of previous resource test results, resource usage historic, QoS historic, equipment logs, anomaly historics,  

Remark: If the diagnostic action is a Resource Order, this will be created via the Resource Order Initialization function.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Network Traffic Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Network Traffic Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Network Traffic Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Network Traffic Management AF groups function that identify and solve traffic problems in the service provider's network. These problems could be the result of failures and outages in the network as well as abnormal increases in load.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Usage Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Usage Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Usage Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Usage Management aggregate function consist in follow-up of the Resource use, based on Resource. It deals with reports and analysis with an on-the-fly analysis (detailed &amp; aggregated) of the resource uses and comparison with the resource capacity management and with the resource usage analysis forecasts.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Fraud Detection -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Fraud Detection">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Fraud Detection</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Fraud Detection Aggregate Function identifies and prevents fraudulent use of networks or systems.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Usage Collection -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Usage Collection">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Usage Collection</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Resource Usage Collection Aggregate Function collects data from traffic counters and resource usage records which enables gathering of information from equipment and probes.
Examples: 
          - Traffic data (such as switch load counters),
          - Usage data (such as Radius ticket).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Usage Errors Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Usage Errors Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Usage Errors Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Usage Errors Management Aggregate Function manages usage records which have been identified as being non-processable due to edit, guide, routing, etc. Failures
It is in charge of categorizing, analysing root cause, correcting and new submission of Resource Usage failures.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Resource Usage Mediation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Resource Usage Mediation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Resource Usage Mediation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Usage Mediation Aggregate Function correlates data from traffic to identify Resource usages without duplicate entry.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.AI Model Manager -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.AI Model Manager">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>AI Model Manager</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">AI Model Manager Aggregate function group functions to manage and maintain Artificial Intelligence models. It provides functions to store, version, deploy and catalog AI models.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.World Simulator -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.World Simulator">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>World Simulator</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">World Simulator Aggregate function group functions to understand and mimic real-world processes or systems.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Strategy Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Strategy Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Strategy Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Strategy Analysis aggregate function groups functions that manage analysis from Business Partners such as risk analysis.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Financial Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Financial Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Financial Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Financial Analysis Aggregate Function identifies potential financial risks associated with partners and calculates a risk score for each of the partners.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Purchasing Strategy Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Purchasing Strategy Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Purchasing Strategy Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Purchasing Strategy Analysis aggregate function functions groups functions in charge of analyzing purchasing application and sourcing savings.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Purchasing Implementation Analysis -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Purchasing Implementation Analysis">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Purchasing Implementation Analysis</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Purchasing Implementation Analysis Aggregate Function evaluates the purchasing implementation according to the Purchasing Strategy defined.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Sourcing Savings Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Sourcing Savings Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Sourcing Savings Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Sourcing Savings Management Aggregate Function is part of Purchasing process and aims to get cost reduction and/or cost avoidance.
It consists in comparing estimated budget with approved purchase order payments. 

Cost reductions, understood as tangible bottom line reductions, are easily defined as / characterized by: 
-  year-on-year saving over the constant volume of purchased product/service,
-  direct reduction of expense liked to changes in processes/technology/policies that directly reduces expenses, 
-  process improvements that result in real and measurable cost or asset reductions, 
-  examination of existing products or services, contractual agreements, or processes to determine potential changes that reduce cost, and 
-  net reductions in prices paid for items procured when compared to prices in place for the prior 12 months or a change to lower cost alternatives. 

Cost avoidance is much more difficult to define. Suggested definitions include: 
-  avoidance is a cost reduction that does not lower the cost of products/ but rather minimizes or avoids entirely the negative impact to the bottom line that a price increase would have caused, 
-  when there is an increase in output/capacity without increasing resource expenditure, in general, the cost avoidance savings are the amount that would have been spent to handle the increased volume/output, 
- avoidances include process improvements that do not immediately reduce cost or assets but provide benefits through improved process efficiency, employee productivity, improved customer satisfaction, improved competitiveness, etc.; over time, cost avoidance often becomes cost savings.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Purchasing Strategy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Purchasing Strategy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Purchasing Strategy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Purchasing Strategy Management aggregate function functions groups functions in charge of defining Purchasing Strategy, Purchasing needs forecast and purchasing request management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Purchasing Tender Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Purchasing Tender Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Purchasing Tender Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Purchasing Tender Management Aggregate Function is in charge of defining tenders, triggering call for tender, and validating partners accepted for tenders.
It includes RFI (Request for information), RFQ (Request for Quotation) with valuation and RFP (Request for Proposal) for the proposal of estimate, these various stages can be made for a particular project or within the framework of group purchases.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Purchasing Strategy Definition -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Purchasing Strategy Definition">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Purchasing Strategy Definition</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Purchasing Strategy Definition Aggregate Function develops Business Partner Purchasing Strategy according to Partner collaboration constraints. It also analyses partner strategy observance.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Purchasing Needs Forecast -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Purchasing Needs Forecast">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Purchasing Needs Forecast</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Purchasing Needs Forecasts Aggregate Function gathers the forecasts from the users.
It permits to define external purchases volume by perimeter and country according to business strategic priorities (make or buy), budget constraints or technical constraints.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Onboarding -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Onboarding">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Onboarding</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Onboarding AF groups functions that enable the integration and/or interaction of the systems. processes, and personnel of business partners with those of a Service Provider.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Product Specification and Offering Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Product Specification and Offering Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Product Specification and Offering Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Product Specification and Offering Management aggregate function groups functions that manage Business Partner's Product Specification and Offering onboarding and request for quotation.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Product Specification and Offering Onboarding -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Product Specification and Offering Onboarding">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Product Specification and Offering Onboarding</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Product Specification and Offering Onboarding Aggregate Function collects information from Business Partners to onboard their Product Specifications and Offerings.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Business Partner Management aggregate function functions enable the creation and updates of Business Partner information such as name, address, organization, site location, interlocutors and interlocutor roles (legal representatives). 
The term Business Partner is used in the broad sense: dealer, reseller, wholesales, content provider (e.g. TV on XDSL), other network operator for interconnection or roaming purpose, Logistic Operator, partner, service provider for the technical support and IT projects, etc.
When the CSP decides to work with a Business Partner, he is referenced and typified (Supplier or Supplier and Partner, or with regard to the contents of purchases). 
It also manages supplier lists, preferred suppliers lists and also suppliers' black list, with information such as name, address, etc.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Agreement Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Agreement Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Agreement Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Business Partner Agreement Management Aggregate Function includes supplier products and offers description, including prices, negotiated with the supplier according to the RFP (RFQ) content.
These agreements can be more or less complex and include:
- Purchases of materials and/or service deliveries, with prices depending on quantity, volume discounts or penalties, etc.
- Revenue sharing conditions, depending on end-customers usages for example.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Role Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Role Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Role Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Business Partner Role Repository Management Aggregate Function enables to identify the relevant roles associated with a Party during the commercial relationship with a Supplier. 
The term Supplier is used in the broad sense: dealer, reseller, wholesales, content provider (e.g. TV on XDSL), other network operator for interconnection or roaming purpose, Logistic Operator, partner, service provider for the technical support and IT projects, 
When we decide to work with a supplier, he is referenced and typified (Supplier or Supplier and Partner, or with regard to the contents of purchases). Partner is a particular typology of Supplier.

The Business Partner Repository Management is also in charge of gathering financial information related to potential partners, partner groups and hierarchies.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Collect and Qualify Business Partner Information -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Collect and Qualify Business Partner Information">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Collect and Qualify Business Partner Information</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Collect and Qualify Business Partner Information Aggregate Function concerns the management of our knowledge of the Business Partner to keep or produce an up-to-date, accurate and legally compliant Business Partner information view.
It will incorporate into the Business Partner Information view, all relevant information gathered through all contacts with the Business Partner and in particular his field of activity.
Business Partner Information from other sources may be gathered, which will build a better knowledge of the supplier.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Product Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Product Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Product Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Business Partner Product Repository Management Aggregate Function allows to manage the configuration and statuses of the offers and products ordered to the Business Partner, according to its Offer and Product catalogue descriptions and rules. It also manages relationships between Products bought to the Business Partner with the CSP's different level of installed base (Product, Know-How a.k.a CFS, Technical Solution a.k.a RFS and Resource).

This Aggregate Function allows:
-	The Order Capture process to instantiate or update offers and products ordered to the Business Partner, whatever their type (network product, bundle, device, ) or their marketing mode (rented, sold, ), with their configuration, their tariffs and discounts, and their status (initialized with a creation order)
-	The Order Delivery to update Offer and Product installed base status
-	Any process to read Offer and Product installed base (subscribed offers, configuration of installed products, installed tariffs and discount, statuses, ).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Order Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Order Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Order Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Order Management Aggregate Function groups functions that manage the order to supplier or partner and the control of their fulfillment.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Order Validation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Order Validation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Order Validation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Order Validation Aggregate Function receives Purchase Requisition, manages approvals and convert into Business Partner Order according to agreed commercial arrangements with Business Partner.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Order Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Order Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Order Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Business Partner Order Repository Management Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to the Business Partner Order and to its items (e.g. : Order lines).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Goods Receipt -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Goods Receipt">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Goods Receipt</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Goods Receipt Aggregate Function manages suppliers goods receipt including goods receipt checking (quantity, working) and possible return.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Performance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Performance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Performance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Business Partner Performance Management aggregate function groups functions used to evaluate Business Partners performance such as Supplier Performance.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Performance Evaluation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Performance Evaluation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Performance Evaluation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The main goal of the Business Partner Performance Evaluation Aggregate Function is to reduce purchase risk and maximize the overall value for the purchaser. It typically involves evaluation, at a minimum, business partner quality, cost competitiveness, potential delivery performance and technological capability. Some of the other criteria used in the preliminary evaluation of business partners include financial risk analysis, evaluation of previous performance, and evaluation of business partner provided information.
Other criteria on the ethics and the environment can be also taken into account.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Trouble Ticket Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Trouble Ticket Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Trouble Ticket Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Trouble Ticket Management Aggregate Function manages Business Partner Trouble Tickets, sending and receiving information about BP Trouble tickets. It includes billing and settlement disputes.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Financial Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Financial Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Financial Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Business Partner Financial Management aggregate function groups functions used to pay Business Partners.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Payment Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Payment Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Payment Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Payment Management Aggregate Function handles payments in the area of Business Partner billing and settlement. It is in charge of implementing Payment according to due debt and assigning in bound as well as outbound payments to invoices and settlement.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Due Debt Matching -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Due Debt Matching">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Due Debt Matching</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Due Debt Matching Aggregate Function permits, prior to payments, to perform controls matching the invoice, sent by the supplier, to the purchase orders or the planned invoice / settlement calculated by the CSP.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Invoice Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Invoice Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Invoice Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Invoice Management aggregate function groups functions which manage the financial interface with the supplier or partner including invoice matching and settlement.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Invoice Matching -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Invoice Matching">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Invoice Matching</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Business Partner Invoice Matching Aggregate Function permits, prior to payments, to perform controls matching the invoice, sent by the business partner, to the purchase orders over the period considered.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Invoicing -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Invoicing">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Invoicing</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Business Partner Invoicing Aggregate Function produces invoices on behalf of a partner, for example in case of revenue sharing. These invoices represent negative revenue for the CSP and will allow the partner for cashing the corresponding payment.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Settlement Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Settlement Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Settlement Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Settlement Management Aggregate Function is necessary when enterprises want to avoid multiple two-way financial flows, for example when : 
- the agreement with the supplier includes revenue sharing (ex : N&#176; contact)
- and/or when the CSP sells on behalf and collects the money for the partner. 
It permits invoices aggregation per partner (received invoices and produced invoices) using a settlement account. 
According to the balance amount of the settlement account, a payment is sent or requested by the CSP to the partner.  

Notice: Settlement notes and invoice notes are both sent to the partner even when only one financial transfer is performed.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Rating -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Rating">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Rating</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Rating aggregate function groups functions which manage the rating part of the supplier or partner.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Bill Calculation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Bill Calculation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Bill Calculation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Supplier Bill Calculation Aggregate Function prepares all the bill items necessary for the invoicing process on behalf of a partner. 
It collects, and eventually aggregates, the relevant results of the rating function, for the partner and for a dedicated period. 
It can also involve bill items coming from other providers.
It can calculate penalties or bonus depending on global amounts (of revenue, of usage types, ) or apply complex tax rules.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Rating and Revenue Sharing -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Rating and Revenue Sharing">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Rating and Revenue Sharing</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Rating and Revenue Sharing Aggregate Function is in charge of:

	- Business Partner Revenue Sharing Identification that identifies the relevant pricing rules (tariffs, discounts and penalties) to be applied according to Revenue Sharing Agreement.
	- Business Partner Revenue Sharing Enforcement that applies revenue sharing pricing rules to calculate what the CSP owes to the Business Partner and what is owed by the Business Partner to the CSP.


	- And Business Partner Settlement Management that calculates settlements amounts according to what the CSP owes to the Business Partner (for Offers bought to the BP and for Revenue Sharing) and what is owed by the Business Partner to the CSP (for what has been bought by the BP to the CSP and for Revenue sharing).


It is done for any type of charges:

	-  recurring charges
	-  one-time charges (installation fees,  )
	-  usage charges, based on unitary product usage records (CDR) or on previously aggregated usage records


This information can be necessary to validate the supplier invoice the CSP will receive, or to prepare an invoice on behalf of the partner, for example in case of revenue sharing (ex : settlement of 10% of the revenue billed by the CSP to his customers, for a set of contents, to his supplier).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Welcome and Interaction -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Welcome and Interaction">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Welcome and Interaction</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Welcome and Interaction aggregate function groups functions in charge of managing all interfaces between the company and Business Partners.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Interaction Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Interaction Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Interaction Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Interaction Management Aggregate Function manages all contact events with Business Partners.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Partner Relationship Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Partner Relationship Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Partner Relationship Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Business Partner Relationship Management Aggregate Function identifies potential partners and determines partners that best meet the enterprise's needs.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Financial Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Financial Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Financial Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Financial Management Aggregate Function contains functions that permit budget, treasury as well as employee expenses management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Budget Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Budget Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Budget Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Budget Management Aggregate Function basically includes: budget forecast, simulation and control. Then it permits analysis, organization and oversight of costs and expenditures for the CSP.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Workforce Operational Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Workforce Operational Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Workforce Operational Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">This Aggregate Function groups functions which manage the teams operational detailed workload schedule.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Workforce Activity Tracking -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Workforce Activity Tracking">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Workforce Activity Tracking</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Workforce Activity Tracking Aggregate Function permits on-the-fly analysis of work orders management and workforce activity - including subcontractor's activities. 

It produces reports and KPI such as 
- workload per type of activity, per team or individual
- wait of intervention per type of activity
- short term forecasts of activity, based on effective production 
- etc.
It permits to measure current efficiency and to identify over or under loaded calendars (ex: increase of field interventions on a specific area after storm damage).

These information can help to adapt the short term workforce calendars - through the dedicated function.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Workforce Organization -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Workforce Organization">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Workforce Organization</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Workforce Organization Aggregate Function permits to enrich the description of the actors and the operational teams, with information necessary to apply assignment rules, such as :

	- Detailed skills, relevant for the activity type
	- Assignment to team(s) and availability level
	- Assignment to territories, sales channel, products lines (ex: FTTH, ), customers types (ex: Major Accounts, Mass Market Customers, )
	- etc.
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Workforce Schedule Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Workforce Schedule Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Workforce Schedule Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Workforce Schedule Management Aggregate Function permits to initiate and update staff calendars and availability per type of activity and possible carrying out models - at team and individual levels - according to criteria and rules.

The workload management schedule is first concerned by future requirements and is limited to capacity management - for internal teams and subcontractors. It uses mid-term activity forecasts.
The workload management schedule is then updated and detailed at teams and - if necessary - individuals levels to become operational (ex: for the next 2 weeks).

The scheduled can be adapted depending on Workforce Activity Tracking information. Adaptation may consist in allowing dispatching of staff from other geographical area or to other activities, adapt staff allocation per type of activity, hiring temporary manpower, increase subcontracting volume, </rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Workforce Appointment Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Workforce Appointment Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Workforce Appointment Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Workforce Appointment Management Aggregate Function enables to manage Workforce appointment with any Party such as Customer, Business Partner. It takes into account the availability of the participants and tracks the appointment in the workforce calendar.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Bank Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Bank Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Bank Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Bank Management Aggregate Function is in charge of managing CSP's Bank Accounts.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Treasury Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Treasury Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Treasury Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Treasury Management Aggregate Function consists in managing short or medium term cash-flow forecasts, financial investments for cash surplus as well as cash funding for company requirements.
It embeds the following sub-functions:
- daily cash flow position delivery, at short-term and medium-term
- cash flow budget management to forecast cash requirements or surplus
- certification and transmission of settlements or debit orders towards banks by the means of SWIFT or EBICS messages
- cash flows transfers accounting
- bank reconciliation
- foreign exchange rates management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Enterprise Revenue Assurance -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Enterprise Revenue Assurance">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Enterprise Revenue Assurance</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Revenue Assurance Management Aggregate Function includes functions which are in charge of defining company strategy and methods to minimize the revenue leakage.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Revenue Assurance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Revenue Assurance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Revenue Assurance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Revenue Assurance Aggregate Function handles all the actions having for target to make sure, the procedures and the practices implemented by the company, that all the incomes expected from products supplied to the customers are invoiced, recovered and registered in an exhaustive and exact way.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Revenue Assurance Problem Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Revenue Assurance Problem Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Revenue Assurance Problem Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Revenue Assurance Problem Management Aggregate Function identifies, tracks and solve Revenue Assurance Problems and initiate recovery of the lost revenue.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Revenue Assurance Rules Definition -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Revenue Assurance Rules Definition">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Revenue Assurance Rules Definition</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Revenue Assurance Rules Definition Aggregate Function establishes the policies and rules to be applied to the Revenue Assurance process.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Human Resource Assurance -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Human Resource Assurance">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Human Resource Assurance</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Human Resource Assurance Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of defining, analyzing and reporting short and long terms goals for employees.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Human Resource Performance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Human Resource Performance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Human Resource Performance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Human Resource Performance Management Aggregate Function supports defining and evaluating goals for personnel.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Accounting Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Accounting Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Accounting Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Accounting Management Aggregate Function contains functions which allow one to manage the accounting of the company.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.General Ledger -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.General Ledger">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>General Ledger</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The General Ledger Aggregate Function provides a central repository for all financial transactions and statistical information. It is at the core of accountancy. In general, it permits to define a multi-company, multi-divisional, multi-currency ledger.
- Natural accounts management (e.g. Account 401 to aggregate all the auxiliaries suppliers 401xxx)
- Posting in Control Accounts + other accounts
- Enter journals, upload journals
- Reversal of journals (i.e.: accruals)
- Open and Close GL Periods
- Foreign currency Revaluation and Translation
- Cost and Revenue breakdown
- Reporting and analysis.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Lease Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Lease Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Lease Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Lease Management Aggregate Function manages Capital Lease for Complete contract tracking and reporting.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Subledger Accounting -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Subledger Accounting">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Subledger Accounting</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">An auxiliary account is one subdivision of a general account and concerns a third party account. It leans on an auxiliary journal which collects the credit / debit events. There are:
- a purchase journal
- a sales journal 
- banks journals (one per bank)
- a cash book
- a miscellaneous transaction journal
- a payroll journal.

The auxiliary feeds periodically the general accounting under the shape of a single centralized aggregated book entry. 
The Subledger Accounting Aggregate Function:
- limits the number of book entry by third party account
- facilitates the accounting lettering
- helps to the preparation of the balance sheet
- favors the periodic accounting audit.

It includes the auxiliary Customer, Supplier and Stock accountancies.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Tax Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Tax Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Tax Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Tax Management Aggregate Function assures:
- taxes declarations and particularly VAT 
- legal reports management
- fiscal rules definition and the control of the application of these rules, including by the tax authorities.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Regulatory Accounting -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Regulatory Accounting">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Regulatory Accounting</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Regulatory Accounting Aggregate Function is in charge of managing cost allocations, affiliate billing and international settlements according to Regulatory Accounting rules.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Human Resource Expense Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Human Resource Expense Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Human Resource Expense Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Human Resource Expense Management Aggregate Function cover the treatment of employees expenses such as payroll and travel expenses.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Travel Expense Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Travel Expense Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Travel Expense Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Travel Expense Management Aggregate Function collects Employees' expense including electronic attachments. It also manages the approval of expenses, the expense reimbursements and integration with accounts payable.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Asset Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Asset Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Asset Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Asset Management Aggregate Function mainly enables one to:
- manage all the resources which are used and shared within the company, namely the physical and financial assets deployed in the enterprise
- optimize the expected savings linked to these all along their lifecycle from their purchase and setup until their total depreciation or amortization.

This Aggregate Function embeds three functions:
- Corporate Real Estate Management
- Vehicle Fleet Management
- Intellectual Property Management

This Aggregate Function DOESN'T manage the Tangible and Intangible Products sold to the CUSTOMERS, only those used within the enterprise for its own needs.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Corporate Real Estate (Buildings) -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Corporate Real Estate (Buildings)">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Corporate Real Estate (Buildings)</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Corporate Real Estate Aggregate Function:
- Describes the real estate property held or used by a business enterprise or organization for its own operational purposes.
- May also describe the functional practices, organizations and professions that are concerned with the planning, acquisition, management, and administration of real property on behalf of a company.

Corporate real estate professionals handle real estate market exactly as corporate purchasing or procurement.
It includes the following functions : 
- Real Estate Facility Operations Management (FM) is an interdisciplinary field devoted to the co-ordination of all business support services. FM involves all types of business support processes including the care of offices, commercial or institutional buildings which are necessary to the operator and the employees. FM can be defined as the integration and alignment of the non-core services, including those relating to premises, required to operate and maintain a business to fully support the core objectives of the organization.
- Space planning function deals with the way to plan and organize required necessary space in buildings for operator activities. It embeds: offices, workrooms, shops, warehouses, telecom equipment centers, data centers, switch centers, corporate restaurants, and parking.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Intellectual Property Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Intellectual Property Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Intellectual Property Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Intellectual Property Management Aggregate Function manages the lifecycle of intellectual assets, their use and share.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Vehicle Fleet Management (Transport) -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Vehicle Fleet Management (Transport)">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Vehicle Fleet Management (Transport)</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Vehicle Fleet Management permits to manage company's vehicle fleet. 
Vehicle fleet is made of commercial motor vehicles such as cars, vans, trucks, boats etc.
Fleet (vehicle) management includes a set of functionalities, such as 
- Vehicle financing,
- Vehicle maintenance,
- Vehicle telematics (tracking and diagnostics),
- River management, speed management,
- Fuel management, 
- Health and safety management.

Fleet Management is a function which allows companies which rely on transportation in their business to remove or minimize the risks associated with vehicle investment, improving efficiency, productivity and reducing their overall transportation and staff costs, providing 100% compliance with government legislation (duty of care) and many more.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Loan Securitization AF -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Loan Securitization AF">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Loan Securitization AF</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">To be ranamed</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Enterprise Information Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Enterprise Information Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Enterprise Information Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Enterprise Information Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of managing all knowledge of the Enterprise.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Enterprise Knowledge Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Enterprise Knowledge Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Enterprise Knowledge Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Enterprise Knowledge Management Aggregate Function is in charge of collecting, classifying, and giving access to information useful for the enterprise.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Enterprise Collaboration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Enterprise Collaboration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Enterprise Collaboration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Enterprise Collaboration Aggregate Function groups functions that foster effective communication and cooperation among employees, promoting innovation and productivity. Their responsibilities include:
1.	Facilitating Communication: Enabling seamless communication through various channels, such as email, chat, file sharing, audio and video conferencing.
2.	Knowledge Sharing: Promoting the sharing of information, documents, and best practices across teams and departments.
3.	Project Collaboration: Supporting teamwork by providing tools for collaborative project management and document sharing.
4.	Innovation Support: Creating an environment for ideation, innovation, and cross-functional collaboration.
5.	Workflow Integration: Integrating collaboration tools with other enterprise software to streamline processes.
6.	Security and Compliance: Ensuring data security and compliance with industry regulations.
7.	User Support: Offering training and assistance to employees to maximize the use of collaboration tools.
8.	Performance Metrics: Measuring and improving the effectiveness of collaboration initiatives.
9.	Feedback Collection: Gathering feedback from users to refine and enhance collaboration solutions, ensuring they align with organizational goals and needs.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Enterprise Risk Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Enterprise Risk Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Enterprise Risk Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Enterprise Risk Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of managing Enterprise Ricks realizing audits, managing patent, invention and damage claims.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Damage Risk Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Damage Risk Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Damage Risk Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Damage Risk Management Aggregate Function is in charge of collecting damage claims, calculating consequences (i.e. loss for the company).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Enterprise Audit Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Enterprise Audit Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Enterprise Audit Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Enterprise Audit Management Aggregate Function is in charge of auditing the enterprise according to SOX and tracking the compliance results.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Patent and Invention Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Patent and Invention Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Patent and Invention Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Patent, Invention &amp; Trademark Management collects possible patent, invention and trademark candidates.
It also manages the registration process and making the knowledge available within the enterprise.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Human Resource Administration, Compensation and Benefits Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Human Resource Administration, Compensation and Benefits Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Human Resource Administration, Compensation and Benefits Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Human Resource Administration, Compensation and Benefits Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of
1. processing and managing records and employees data, ensuring they are accurate and up-to-date, corresponding and taking into account any changes resulting from management decisions, legal and regulatory requirements and personal circumstances,
2. managing the individual Compensation and Benefits calculation, giving the instructions for the payroll process.
Note that for some countries (UK), the Information System must provide functions for pension funds and social security management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Human Resource Administration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Human Resource Administration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Human Resource Administration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Human Resource Administration Aggregate Function permits management of all legal and administrative employees data such as identity, contacts/addresses, contract of employment, job position (job profile, localization, management), etc.
Processes the registration of the new employees (hiring, mobility), updates personal situation (marital status, children, addresses, dependents / beneficiaries, etc) and professional situation, updates contracts status (termination, resignation, retirement).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Human Resource Communication Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Human Resource Communication Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Human Resource Communication Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Human Resource Communication Management Aggregate Function supports Corporate Goals and Key initiatives communication and facilitates community programs.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Employee Relationship Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Employee Relationship Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Employee Relationship Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Employee Relationship Management Aggregate Function is in charge of all relationships between CSP and employees including advertising for recruitment.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Individual Remuneration Policy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Individual Remuneration Policy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Individual Remuneration Policy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Individual Remuneration Policy Aggregate Function manages the employees individual remuneration.
It represents the declension at employee level of the general company remuneration policy (fixed base salary, managerial bonus), the standard benefits and all other kind of benefits granted to the employee (profit sharing, stock options, shareholders program, car policy).

Subfunctions used:
- Compensation Management,
- Profit Sharing Management,
- Benefits Management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Payroll Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Payroll Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Payroll Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Payroll Aggregate Function permits to manage:
- employee payroll data applying the Individual Remuneration Policy and ensuring they are accurate and up-to-date, reflecting any changes resulting from management decisions, legal &amp; regulatory requirements and personal circumstances in order to provide payslips, payment, contributions, legal declarations, etc.
- the calculation of the payroll data
- the payment of the payroll.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Employee Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Employee Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Employee Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Employee Repository Management Aggregate Function is in charge of managing employees information and identification means such as unique employee number and identification card.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Social Relationships Piloting -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Social Relationships Piloting">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Social Relationships Piloting</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Social Relationships Piloting Aggregate Function covers the piloting of the relationships with the employees representatives.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Working Time Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Working Time Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Working Time Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Working Time Administration Aggregate Function manages all elements associated with time worked including overtime, night, weekend and home works management.
This function provides a consolidated situation of employees working time.
It allows the management of the working schedule, the overtime hours, the penalties, </rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Human Resource Human Capital Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Human Resource Human Capital Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Human Resource Human Capital Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Human Resource Human Capital Management Aggregate Function groups functions covering all the operations and data related to human resource and competencies development.
It covers, on one hand, the internal and external processes of recruitment, and, on the other hand, the processes contributing in the management of the performance and competencies which aims to improve individual performance and, by this way, to insure the achievement of Orange group's strategic business goals.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Evaluations and Career Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Evaluations and Career Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Evaluations and Career Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Evaluation and Career Management Aggregate Function treats about individual management of career and performance of an employee.
Two main goals:
- First: improve individual performance of each employee all along his professional career
This goal relies on assessment processes consisting in a regular face to face meeting between employee and his manager in order to:

	- share and agree on individual goals,
	- measure employee performance results,
	- assess employee competencies and then, 
	-  identify training development actions.

- Second: manage and propose development career plan based upon competencies management 

	-  proceeding  to a follow-up of individual career data details to see and track individual career evolution,
	-  helping in displaying to employee internally needs that could match to employee wishes.


It is based upon a shared group master data referential managing Jobs and Competencies that is updated according to company policy strategy.
It gathers the following sub-functions:
- Setting (and sharing) individual Goals,
- Performance individual assessment,
- Company Jobs and Skills master data management 
- Gap assessment between awaited and owned employee competencies 
- Employee assignment to key company jobs.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.External and Internal (Mobility) Recruitment -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.External and Internal (Mobility) Recruitment">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>External and Internal (Mobility) Recruitment</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The External and Internal (Mobility) Recruitment Aggregate Function is in charge of finding, allocating and making ready the right resources at the right time to target areas of the business.
It consumes and stores standard CVs and provides services permitting selection of profiles (including interviews results, candidate choice). 
This function also provides job offer search and display. This function includes the mobility for internal applicants and covers internal mobility inside the same company of the CSP group or between companies of the CSP Group. Special transfers to national and local government services are also taken into account by this function. 
It includes Job Offer Management, Applicant Management, Job Application Management and Applicant Pre-Hiring.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Training Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Training Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Training Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Training Management Aggregate Function addresses and manages the training functions in its globality from training elaboration and delivery to course assessment after course attending.
It permits to manage employee inscription to course session, courses assessment, etc 

It embeds the following sub-functions:
- Training plan management, 
- Training catalogue management, 
- Training Course session inscription management,
- External training purchasing
- Training delivery management.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Human Resource Social Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Human Resource Social Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Human Resource Social Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Human Resource Social Management Aggregate Function groups functions covering all the operations and data related to social management.
It defines the policy for employees relations, health and safety, and corporate social responsibility. It supports managers in their implementation and control results.
Each company / country develops its own approach in employees relations, health and safety, work time and corporate social responsibility in coherence with group policies and local legal and mandatory requirements and agreements.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Safety and Working Environment Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Safety and Working Environment Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Safety and Working Environment Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">This Aggregate Function permits to manage all the data necessary to ensure an even improving safety and working environment in compliance with legal requirements.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Social Protection Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Social Protection Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Social Protection Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">This Aggregate Function covers the management or the taking account of the social protection systems: life insurance, disability insurances, employers liability insurances, occupational diseases insurances.
This function doesnt cover the financial part of the contracts and funds functioning operations. In all the countries, these operations and the associated managing system are directly provided by insurance companies.
Nevertheless, in some countries (UK), the CSP HR actors have to make specific operations and controls about social security contribution. These operations and controls are covered by the Social security management function in the HR Administration functional block.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Ethics & Compliance Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Ethics & Compliance Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Ethics & Compliance Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Ethics &amp; Compliance Management provides the following functionality:
- Provides a program to ensure Labor is properly trained on ethics and compliance requirements. 
- Provides a program to enable safe reporting of ethical and compliance violations. 
- Ensures company is meeting ethical and compliance standards. 
- Institutes and manages programs such as EEO case management to track violations.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Regulatory Rules Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Regulatory Rules Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Regulatory Rules Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Regulatory Rules Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of managing regulatory rules, checking their application by the enterprise and providing reporting of regulatory rules to various legal and regulatory entities.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Legal Support -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Legal Support">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Legal Support</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Legal Support Aggregate Function assists and advices administrative legal services for legal issues such as procurement and public law.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Regulatory Rules Application Tracking -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Regulatory Rules Application Tracking">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Regulatory Rules Application Tracking</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Regulatory Rules Application Tracking Aggregate Function tracks and reports the implementation of regulatory rules or requirements.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Regulatory Rules Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Regulatory Rules Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Regulatory Rules Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Regulatory Rules Repository Management Aggregate Function manages Regulatory Rules and proof of conformance to the rules by the CSP.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Security Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Security Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Security Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Security Management Aggregate Function includes functions in charge of defining company strategy and means to improve security.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Lawful Interception -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Lawful Interception">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Lawful Interception</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Lawful Interception Aggregate Function permits to ensure compliance with legal obligations by providing analyses or evidence of traffic and usage.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Security Strategy Definition and Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Security Strategy Definition and Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Security Strategy Definition and Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Security Strategy Definition and Management function allows to better anticipate, understand and prevent risks, related to the security of people, assets and information by defining the CSP Security Policy.

It includes: 
- The identification of risk and risk level on various security aspects
- The definition and implementation of security rules and the means to verify compliance (processes, tools, organisation, etc.).
- The management of major crises (technical, environmental, etc.)
- The management of Safety Audits
- The security context monitoring

Security concerns several parts: 
- CSP financial and legal security
- Personnel security (employees in their work environment, customers)
- Site security (buildings)
- Network, Service Platform and IS infrastructure security
- Information security (GDPR, confidentiality, defence secrecy)

The CSP Security Policy may be applied to these different parts, and its implementation may be carried out in operational functions of collection, control, supervision and analysis.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Privacy Development -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Privacy Development">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Privacy Development</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Privacy Development Aggregate Function are in charge of specifying Privacy Rules to be applied.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Privacy Definition  Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Privacy Definition  Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Privacy Definition  Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Privacy Definition Management Aggregate Function is in charge of specifying Privacy Profile Types and related rules, possible values and default configuration.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Privacy Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Privacy Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Privacy Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Privacy Management Aggregate Function are in charge of applying the Privacy Rules when required.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Privacy Control -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Privacy Control">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Privacy Control</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Privacy Control Aggregate Function controls that records retention rules are applied as well as privacy rules.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Privacy Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Privacy Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Privacy Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Privacy Repository Management Aggregate Function manages Privacy Profiles initialized by default or customized by the Party and obtains consent from the Data Subject Party for the Party Profile.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Content Delivery Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Content Delivery Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Content Delivery Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Content Delivery AF groups functions that ensuring the effective and efficient dissemination of digital content to end-users. Their responsibilities include:
1.	Content Distribution: Efficiently delivering digital content to end-users across various platforms and devices (Source: "Content Delivery Network (CDN)" - TechTarget).
2.	Load Balancing: Ensuring even distribution of network traffic to prevent overloads and improve performance.
3.	Caching Management: Implementing cache strategies to reduce load times and optimize content delivery.
4.	Traffic Routing: Directing user requests to the nearest server for faster content access.
5.	Security: Protecting content and networks from cyber threats and DDoS attacks .
6.	Quality of Service: Maintaining high-quality content delivery, including minimizing latency and packet loss 
7.	Scalability: Ensuring the system can handle increased demand without performance degradation
8.	Monitoring and Analytics: Constantly tracking content delivery performance and analyzing data for optimization.
9.	Content Optimization: Compressing, transcoding, or adapting content for better delivery to various devices.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Anomaly Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Anomaly Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Anomaly Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Anomaly Management Aggregate Function groups functions that detect, predict, mitigate and maintain knowledge of anomalies for products, services and/or resources.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Anomaly Detection -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Anomaly Detection">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Anomaly Detection</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Anomaly Detection Aggregate function group functions to identify rare items, events, or observations for products, services or resources which deviate significantly from well-defined notion of normal behavior.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Anomaly Prediction -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Anomaly Prediction">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Anomaly Prediction</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Anomaly Prediction Aggregate function group functions to forecast future anomalies based on historical data.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Anomaly Mitigation -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Anomaly Mitigation">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Anomaly Mitigation</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Anomaly Mitigation Aggregate function group functions to define strategies and plan  to reduce the impact or prevent the occurence of detected predicted anomaly.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Content Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Content Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Content Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Content Management Aggregate Function enables documents and contents management, sharing and archiving.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Content Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Content Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Content Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Content Repository Management Aggregate Function is in charge of collecting and formatting contents and managing it collaboratively.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Document Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Document Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Document Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Document Management Aggregate Function handles creation and management of electronic documents.
Documents managed by an EDM (Electronic Document Management) system are both working documents, which can be modified, and final documents. The sub-function Versioning allows the identification of successive releases of the document. Documents are accessed by the user according to the granted access rights.
Subfunctions :
- Capture and integration in the EDM system (for examples GED, sharepoint, social network, )
- Retention and disposal
- Access and grant control
- Traceability, security and administration
- Versioning.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Records Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Records Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Records Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Records Management Aggregate Function handles document and data to be archived.
Documents or data which must be archived are those which are valuable and useful for the company:
- Legal value: information that must be preserved for reasons that are legal (when a law requires retention) and regulatory or judicial (proof in court).
- Informational value: information that must be preserved for professional needs and for the smooth conduct of business activities.
- Historical value: information preserved for its contribution to the collective company memory.
All of these documents and data must be sorted and classified according to their level of sensitivity, preserved (or destroyed) in a structured and secure manner.
The Records Management Aggregate Function contains the 4 sub-functions:
1) Records Capture
2) Record Access
3) Records Retention and Disposal
4) Records Traceability, Security and Administration</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Master Data Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Master Data Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Master Data Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Master Data Management Aggregate Function gathers all required functions for enterprise master data administration. It aims at unifying data repositories management whatever the business domain.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Data Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Data Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Data Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Data Repository Management Aggregate Function is in charge of all activities od Data management such as formatting, security access, distribution, archiving</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Data Structure Definition -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Data Structure Definition">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Data Structure Definition</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Data Structure Definition Aggregate Function defines Data Structure and related rules.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Project Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Project Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Project Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Project Management Aggregate Function contains functions concerning the management of all kinds of projects.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Project Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Project Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Project Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Project Repository Management Aggregate Function is in charge of managing the Project Inventory and produces reports and KPI to measure the progress of the project.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Project Planning -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Project Planning">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Project Planning</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Project Planning Aggregate Function handles all functions associated with the project management and not already implemented in other Aggregate Functions (resources management, budget management, ), that facilitate the work of the project managers.
Sub-functions used:
- Tasks and activities Building:
Based on a multi-level Work Breakdown Structure (WBS), this function allows to define, organize and interface all activities of the project necessary to meet the predefined objectives of the project: delivery of the agreed scope (offer, product, ), quality, time and cost constraints.
- Project Planning:
Management of the project estimating, the means of the project optimizing, tasks schedule and identifying the critical path, evaluating and re affecting task over resources.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Identification and Permission Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Identification and Permission Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Identification and Permission Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Identification and Permission Management Aggregate Function consist in 

	- identifying, and potentially authenticating, a person whatever his/her role(s),
	- specifying roles and permissions definition, assignment and control for any actor implicated in the enterprise business processes (ex: customer, CSR, data protection manager, Business Partners, etc.)
</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Identification and Authentication -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Identification and Authentication">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Identification and Authentication</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Identification and Authentication Aggregate Function consists in identifying, and potentially authenticating, a person whatever his/her role. 
Authentication is not systematic but may be required for sensitive actions according to security matters.
Identification and authentication means may vary depending on the interaction channel. For automatic interaction channels, the means of identification and authentication are highly discriminant and should return at more one result (usually using personal credentials). For human interaction channels, on the other hand, a series of information may be necessary to identify and authenticate a person (for instance asking their name and first name for identification followed by birth date and birth place for authentication).</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Permission Control -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Permission Control">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Permission Control</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Permission Control Aggregate Function manages roles and related permissions and controls permissions according to roles and related rules.
It consists in evaluating if a requester is granted the permission to act by providing the required evidence. The evidence corresponds to the condition specified for each right (for instance keying the correct password to use a specific mailbox). If the action is protected via a right which is assigned (possibly via a role) to a person then the person has to be identified to retrieve their rights and verify if the request to act can be granted.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Roles & Permission Definition -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Roles & Permission Definition">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Roles & Permission Definition</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Roles &amp; Permission Definition Aggregate Function defines roles types and possible permissions with rules according to roles.
Roles catalogue consists in designing, creating, modifying and deleting role specifications. The aim is to identify the set of permissions (rights) that should be granted to any person who is assigned the role. Defining roles depends on processes and associated organization (internal or external), for example a fleet manager (external role) or a CSR level 1 (internal). As part of roles definitions, incompatibility in between roles or other types of constraints should also be specified. The purpose of a role is to ease the assignment of rights to a person by avoiding rights reassignment in case of re organization for instance.
Permission catalogue consists in designing, creating, modifying and deleting permission specifications. A permission specifies the conditions to access a set of actions on an object. For example, in order to access all usage functions of a mailbox product the condition is to provide the correct password.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Digital Identity Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Digital Identity Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Digital Identity Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Digital Identity Management Aggregate Function is in charge of providing  Digital Identities with their credentials and relationship with Party or Party Roles.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Role and Permission Assignment / Configuration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Role and Permission Assignment / Configuration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Role and Permission Assignment / Configuration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Role and Permission Assignment / Configuration Aggregate Function consists in:

	- assigning a party role to a person according to a type of role such as Customer or Payer. Sometimes a party role might be not played by a Party when unknown. For example, we might not know the Party that uses a mail box.
	- specifying on which perimeter the permission carried by the Party Role applies on. A perimeter defines:

   - either an object or a set of objects such as a Product, a Billing Account, etc.
   - or using selection criteria to identify objects on which permission applies such as geographical area where the Distributor has right to sell</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Fallout Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Fallout Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Fallout Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Fallout Management Aggregate Function is in charge of fixing Fallout errors and managing Fallout inventory.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Fallout Correction Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Fallout Correction Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Fallout Correction Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Fallout Correction Management Aggregate Function is in charge of fixing Fallout errors either automatically or by queuing the Fallout error.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Fallout Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Fallout Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Fallout Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Fallout Repository Management Aggregate Function is in charge of managing the Fallout Inventory, including orchestration, reporting and dashboard.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Party Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Party Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Party Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Party Management Aggregate Function includes functions that manage information describing the Third Parties, individuals or organizations, and their contact medium.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Party Repository Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Party Repository Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Party Repository Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Party Repository Management Aggregate Function allows the creation, modification, deletion of a Third Party (induvidual or organization).
For people, it allows to declare their marital status and official proof of identity.
For an organization (company, association, etc.), it manages its corporate name, the description of its internal organization according to several axes (legal, functional, etc.), its business justification (Kbis in France, etc.).
Party Management also includes the management of the life cycle of parties.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Systems and Applications Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Systems and Applications Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Systems and Applications Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Systems and Applications Management AF groups functions responsible for managing IT systems and applications, from Planning though Retirement, including operations.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Systems Administration -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Systems Administration">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Systems Administration</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">System Administration AF groups functions that involve managing and maintaining computer systems, networks, and servers to ensure their optimal performance, security, and reliability. The principal responsibilities include:
1.	Installing and configuring operating systems and software.
2.	Managing user accounts and access permissions.
3.	Monitoring system performance and resource utilization.
4.	Implementing security measures to safeguard data and networks.
5.	Performing backups and disaster recovery planning.
6.	Troubleshooting and resolving technical issues.
7.	Applying software updates and patches.
8.	Documenting system configurations and procedures.
9.	Capacity planning for future system growth.
10.	Ensuring compliance with industry standards and best practices.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Systems Infrastructure Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Systems Infrastructure Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Systems Infrastructure Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Systems Infrastructure Management AF groups functions responsible for overseeing the foundational technology components critical to an organization's operations. Its key responsibilities include:
1.	Infrastructure Planning: Strategically designing and scaling the IT infrastructure to meet business needs.
2.	Hardware Procurement: Sourcing, purchasing, and maintaining server, storage, and networking equipment.
3.	Software Integration: Ensuring seamless integration of software solutions across the infrastructure.
4.	Security Management: Safeguarding systems and data through security measures and protocols.
5.	Capacity Optimization: Efficiently utilizing resources to meet performance demands.
6.	Disaster Recovery: Developing and testing plans for data and system recovery.
7.	Network Management: Managing network connectivity and reliability.
8.	Performance Monitoring: Constantly assessing system performance and addressing issues.
9.	Vendor Relations: Managing relationships with technology suppliers and service providers.
10.	Compliance and Documentation: Ensuring adherence to industry standards and maintaining comprehensive system documentation</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Systems and Applications Support -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Systems and Applications Support">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Systems and Applications Support</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Systems and Applications Support function plays a critical role in ensuring the smooth operation of software systems within an organization. Its responsibilities include:
1.	Troubleshooting: Identifying and resolving software issues and user problems.
2.	Maintenance: Regularly updating, patching, and optimizing applications.
3.	Monitoring: Keeping a watchful eye on system performance and responding to anomalies.
4.	Documentation: Maintaining comprehensive records of configurations and procedures.
5.	User Assistance: Offering guidance and assistance to users facing technical difficulties.
6.	Incident Management: Managing and resolving software-related incidents promptly.
7.	Release Management: Coordinating software updates, ensuring a smooth transition.
8.	Compliance: Ensuring applications adhere to regulatory and security standards.
9.	Vendor Liaison: Interacting with software vendors for support and issue resolution.
10.	Performance Analysis: Analyzing application performance data to drive improvements</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Applications Portfolio Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Applications Portfolio Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Applications Portfolio Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Application Portfolio Management (APM) AF groups functions responsible for overseeing an organization's software and application assets to optimize their performance and strategic alignment. Its principal responsibilities include:
1.	Inventory Management: Maintain a comprehensive inventory of all applications and software within the organization.
2.	Strategic Alignment: Ensure that applications align with the organization's goals and objectives.
3.	Performance Assessment: Evaluate application performance, efficiency, and effectiveness.
4.	Rationalization: Identify redundant or outdated applications and recommend consolidation or retirement.
5.	Prioritization: Rank applications based on their strategic importance and business impact.
6.	Risk Management: Assess and mitigate risks associated with the application portfolio.
7.	Cost Control: Monitor and control application-related costs, aiming for efficiency.
8.	Roadmapping: Develop a strategic roadmap for the application portfolio, including upgrades and replacements.
9.	Vendor Management: Oversee relationships with third-party vendors and software providers.
10.	Reporting and Communication: Provide regular reports and communicate the status and value of the application portfolio to stakeholders.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Data Center Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Data Center Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Data Center Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Data Center Management AF includes functions responsible for overseeing critical infrastructure and ensuring efficient operations within a data center environment. Its principal responsibilities include:
1.	Infrastructure Maintenance: Managing and maintaining hardware, power, and cooling systems to ensure optimal performance.
2.	Security Management: Implementing security measures to safeguard data, equipment, and personnel.
3.	Capacity Planning: Evaluating resource needs and scalability to meet current and future demands.
4.	Disaster Recovery: Developing and testing contingency plans to minimize downtime during emergencies.
5.	Energy Efficiency: Implementing energy-saving measures to reduce operational costs and environmental impact.
6.	Compliance: Ensuring adherence to industry regulations and standards for data center operations.
7.	Performance Monitoring: Continuously tracking and optimizing system performance and uptime.
8.	Asset Inventory: Maintaining an accurate inventory of all data center equipment and resources.
9.	Budget Management: Managing financial resources to meet operational needs and cost-efficiency goals.
10.	Documentation: Maintaining detailed records and documentation for operational transparency and troubleshooting.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Technology Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Technology Management">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Technology Management</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Technology Management AF groups functions responsible for the investigation,lifecycle management , and utilization oversight of technologies and technology approaches.  These responsibilities include:
1.	Strategic Planning: Developing technology strategies aligned with business goals.
2.	Resource Allocation: Managing budgets and resources for tech initiatives.
3.	Risk Assessment: Identifying and mitigating technology-related risks.
4.	Rollout: Managing the introduction and adoption of technologies
5.	Innovation Oversight: Promoting and implementing innovative tech solutions.
6.	Vendor Management: Managing relationships with technology vendors.
7.	Cybersecurity: Protecting digital assets and data from threats.
8.	Compliance: Ensuring adherence to legal and regulatory tech standards.
9.	Lifecycle management: Tracking and coordinating the progress of technologies employed within the organization through the lifecycle stages (including piloting, adoption, growth, sunsetting and retirement) of technologies and technology implementations.
10.	Utilization oversight: Planning, monitoring and adjusting for the acceptance of and utilization of technologies.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Data Analytics -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Data Analytics">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Data Analytics</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Data Analytics aggregate function bridges the gap between raw data and actionable insights, enabling organizations to make data-informed choices and enhance their competitiveness.  It groups functions involving the collection, interpretation, and utilization of data to drive informed decision-making and optimize business strategies. Its responsibilities encompass:
1.	Data Collection: Gathering and consolidating data from various sources.
2.	Data Processing: Cleaning, transforming, and structuring data for analysis.
3.	Data Analysis: Employing statistical and computational techniques to extract insights.
4.	Performance Metrics: Defining and tracking key performance indicators (KPIs).
5.	Predictive Modeling: Building models to forecast trends and outcomes.
6.	Data Visualization: Presenting findings through graphs, charts, and dashboards.
7.	Reporting: Communicating analysis results to stakeholders.
8.	A/B Testing: Conducting experiments to evaluate changes or hypotheses.
9.	Business Intelligence: Supporting strategic decisions with data-driven insights.
10.	Continuous Improvement: Enhancing data analytics processes and tools.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Business Intelligence -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Business Intelligence">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Business Intelligence</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Business Intelligence aggregate function groups functions that leverage data to inform decision-making and drive strategic insights. Its responsibilities include:
1.	Data Gathering: Collecting and aggregating data from various sources.
2.	Data Analysis: Analyzing data to identify trends, patterns, and key insights.
3.	Reporting: Creating reports and dashboards for stakeholders.
4.	Forecasting: Using historical data to predict future trends.
5.	Data Visualization: Presenting data in a visually understandable format.
6.	Performance Metrics: Developing KPIs to measure business success.
7.	Competitive Analysis: Evaluating the organization's position in the market.
8.	Decision Support: Providing data-driven insights for informed decision-making.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Analytics Information Support -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Analytics Information Support">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Analytics Information Support</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">The Analytics Information Support aggregate function groups functions involved in the collection, preparation and organization of information so that it can be used efficently and effectively in data analytics.  Their responsibilities include:
1.	Data Collection: Gathering, organizing, and storing relevant data from various sources.
2.	Data Quality Assurance: Ensuring the accuracy and reliability of collected data.
3. 	Reference and Metadata Management: Defining reference data and dimensions that can be used to express the data consistently
4.	Data Transformation: Converting raw data into usable formats for analysis.
5.	Database Management: Maintaining data repositories and optimizing data access.
6.	Data Security: Implementing safeguards to protect sensitive information.
7.	Troubleshooting: Resolving data-related issues and providing technical support.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Pattern Detection -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Pattern Detection">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Pattern Detection</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Pattern Detection aggregates function group functions to recognize patterns in data.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Communications -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Communications">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Communications</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">TBA</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Natural Language Processor -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Natural Language Processor">
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF"/>
<rdf:label>Natural Language Processor</rdf:label>
<rdfs:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Natural Language Processor Aggregate function group functions to understand, interpret and generate human language, including speech and text.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:Class>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/v1#AF.Lifecycle Integration Management -->

<owl:Class rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/FunctionModel_v1#AF.Lifecycle Integration Management">
